insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_beta_arrestin_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-04","rotationIndex":0,"stem":"Regarding the β-Arrestin pathway in opioid receptors:","choices":["Mediates receptor internalisation","Reduces receptor desensitisation","Contributes to respiratory depression","Is reduced in biased agonists","Increases intracellular cAMP"],"correct":[true,false,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p>When a ligand binds to an opioid receptor, it activates two distinct intracellular pathways:</p>\n\n<table style=\"margin-top:20px\">\n  <tr><th>Pathway</th><th>Key Effects</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>G-protein (Gᵢ) pathway</strong></td><td>Inhibition of adenylate cyclase → ↓ cAMP; closure of voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels; opening of voltage-gated K⁺ channels → hyperpolarisation → inhibition of neurotransmitter release</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>β-Arrestin pathway</strong></td><td>Receptor desensitisation and <strong>internalisation</strong> (contributing to tolerance); side effects including <strong>respiratory depression</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p style=\"margin-top:20px\"><strong>Biased agonism</strong> refers to ligands that preferentially activate one pathway over the other. Biased agonists such as <strong>oliceridine</strong> favour the G-protein pathway over the β-Arrestin pathway, potentially resulting in less tolerance and fewer side effects (including less respiratory depression).</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-top:20px\"><strong>G-protein signalling cascade — step by step:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Ligand binds to the extracellular <strong>N-terminus</strong> of the 7-transmembrane Gᵢ-protein coupled receptor</li>\n  <li>Conformational change → intracellular <strong>GDP is exchanged for GTP</strong> on the α-subunit</li>\n  <li>α-GTP <strong>dissociates</strong> from the βγ dimer</li>\n  <li>α-GTP <strong>inhibits adenylate cyclase</strong> → ↓ conversion of ATP to cAMP</li>\n  <li>βγ dimer <strong>closes voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels</strong> and <strong>opens inwardly-rectifying K⁺ channels</strong></li>\n  <li>Net result: membrane <strong>hyperpolarisation</strong> → inhibition of glutamate release and propagation of action potentials in nociceptive fibres</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"color:#7C7068;font-size:13px;margin-top:20px\">Note: all opioid receptors are 7-transmembrane Gᵢ-protein coupled receptors. The transmembrane domain is highly conserved across receptor subtypes (μ, δ, κ); differences occur mainly in the extracellular regions, which accounts for ligand selectivity.</p>\n\n<img src=\"images/opioid-gpcr.png\" alt=\"G-protein coupled receptor signalling diagram showing Gi-protein activation, adenylate cyclase inhibition, and ion channel modulation\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:20px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\">"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_tn_facial_pain_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-04","rotationIndex":1,"question":"A 60-year-old woman with no significant history presents to clinic complaining of left-sided facial pain. She reports sharp, shooting pains lasting 20 seconds to one minute, triggered by eating, brushing her teeth, talking, and light touch to the side of her face. Between episodes there is no ongoing pain. Clinical examination reveals no abnormality in any territory of the trigeminal nerve. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cluster headache","Trigeminal neuropathic pain","Paroxysmal hemicrania","TMJ syndrome","Trigeminal neuralgia"],"correct":4,"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Cluster headache is a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia (TAC), which is associated with autonomic features (e.g. lacrimation, nasal congestion, ptosis) absent in this case.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Trigeminal neuropathic pain presents as a constant pain, and examination will reveal negative and positive neurological signs — the normal examination here makes this diagnosis unlikely.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Paroxysmal hemicrania is a TAC, which is also associated with autonomic features absent in this case.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> TMJ syndrome causes continuous pain that worsens with chewing; examination typically reveals masseter tenderness — neither feature is present here.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Correct.</strong> Trigeminal neuralgia (TN) presents as short-lasting, lancinating, electric shock-like paroxysms in one or more branches of the trigeminal nerve, triggered by light stimuli (eating, talking, touch). The pain-free interval between attacks and a normal neurological examination are characteristic. First-line treatment is pharmacological (carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine). Surgical options — including microvascular decompression — are considered when medical treatment fails, particularly in the presence of a neurovascular conflict on MRI.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_oird_risk_factors_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-05","rotationIndex":2,"stem":"Factors associated with high risk of postoperative respiratory depression include:","choices":["Age of at least 60 years","Male sex","Chronic opioid use","Sleep-disordered breathing","Chronic heart failure"],"correct":[true,true,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. True.</strong> Individuals aged 60 years or older fall into a higher-risk category for opioid-induced respiratory depression (OIRD).</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. True.</strong> The PRODIGY analysis showed that male patients are more likely to experience OIRD compared with women. Being male contributes 8 points in the PRODIGY scoring system.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. False.</strong> Opioid-naïve patients are at greater risk of postoperative respiratory depression. Those on long-term opioids develop tolerance to both the analgesic and respiratory-depressant effects, reducing — but not eliminating — their susceptibility. Chronic users can still develop respiratory depression if they receive excessive doses.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> Sleep-disordered breathing (e.g. obstructive sleep apnoea) is a strong predictor of postoperative respiratory depression following opioid use, as identified in the PRODIGY model.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. True.</strong> Chronic heart failure is also recognised as a contributing risk factor in the PRODIGY model.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_naloxone_factors_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-05","rotationIndex":3,"stem":"Factors that may affect the use of naloxone for opioid-induced respiratory depression include:","choices":["Duration of opioid use","Type of opioid taken in overdose","Development of withdrawal symptoms","Co-intoxication with tranquillisers","Presence of an opioid use disorder (OUD)"],"correct":[false,true,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. False.</strong> The duration of opioid use does not directly alter naloxone''s ability to block opioid receptors. However, individuals accustomed to very large opioid doses may require substantially higher naloxone doses to achieve adequate reversal.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. True.</strong> Naloxone is less effective against opioids with high μ-receptor affinity. Agents such as buprenorphine are considerably harder to antagonise, making reversal more challenging and often requiring higher or repeated doses.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. True.</strong> Large doses of naloxone can precipitate acute withdrawal, manifesting as severe pain in chronic pain patients, or agitation, irritability, abdominal discomfort, and flu-like symptoms in opioid-dependent individuals.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> If the patient has also taken sedative drugs (e.g. benzodiazepines), naloxone will only counteract the opioid component of respiratory depression. The sedative-induced respiratory suppression persists, making full reversal impossible with naloxone alone.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. False.</strong> Having an opioid use disorder does not interfere with naloxone''s pharmacological action. It will still reverse opioid-mediated respiratory depression, although higher doses may be needed depending on the amount and potency of opioid used.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_opioid_ventilation_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-05","rotationIndex":4,"stem":"Effects of opioids on ventilatory physiology include:","choices":["Suppression of activity of the pre-Bötzinger complex","Increased excitatory drive from the parabrachial complex","Reduction of baseline ventilation","Leftward shift of the CO₂ response curve","Reduction of the slope of the hypercapnic ventilatory response"],"correct":[true,false,true,false,true],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. True.</strong> Opioids depress the pre-Bötzinger complex — the key respiratory rhythm generator in the brainstem — thereby reducing central respiratory drive.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. False.</strong> Opioids decrease, rather than increase, excitatory input from the parabrachial/Kölliker-Fuse complex, further blunting respiratory drive.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. True.</strong> Baseline ventilation falls as a consequence of reduced central respiratory drive and blunted peripheral and central chemosensitivity.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. False.</strong> Opioids shift the CO₂ response curve <em>rightwards</em>, meaning a higher PaCO₂ is required to trigger an increase in ventilation.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. True.</strong> Opioids flatten the hypercapnic ventilatory response slope, reflecting a reduced ventilatory response per unit rise in CO₂ — i.e. diminished sensitivity to hypercapnia.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_gabapentin_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":5,"stem":"Gabapentin:","choices":["Has lower oral bioavailability than pregabalin","Acts primarily on GABA-A receptors","Is absorbed via a saturable active transport system in the gut","Reaches peak plasma concentration approximately 30 minutes after oral dosing","Has a dose-dependent reduction in bioavailability at higher doses"],"correct":[true,false,true,false,true],"explanation":"<p>Gabapentin and pregabalin are α2-δ ligands that modulate voltage-gated calcium channels at presynaptic terminals, reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release. Despite their names, they do not act at GABA receptors. Their pharmacokinetics differ importantly, especially in terms of absorption.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Gabapentin has variable and dose-dependent oral bioavailability of approximately 27–60%, whereas pregabalin has linear, higher bioavailability of ~90%. This is a key pharmacokinetic difference between the two drugs.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Despite being named after GABA, gabapentin does not bind to GABA-A or GABA-B receptors. It binds to the α2-δ subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels. Confusing it with a GABAergic agent is a classic examination trap.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — Gabapentin is absorbed via the LAT1 (large neutral amino acid transporter) in the small intestine. This is a saturable system, which is why bioavailability decreases at higher doses — a clinically relevant fact.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — Gabapentin typically reaches peak plasma concentration at approximately 2–3 hours after oral dosing, not 30 minutes. The 30-minute figure is more consistent with rapidly absorbed agents like immediate-release opioids.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Because of the saturable transporter mechanism, as the oral dose increases, the fraction absorbed decreases. This means higher single doses are proportionally less effective — a key reason for TDS rather than once-daily dosing.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_descending_pain_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":6,"stem":"The descending pain inhibitory system:","choices":["Includes the periaqueductal grey matter","Includes the gamma efferent system","Includes the locus coeruleus","Includes the nucleus tractus solitarius","Includes the nucleus raphe magnus"],"correct":[true,false,true,false,true],"explanation":"<p>The descending inhibitory pain system modulates nociceptive transmission at the level of the dorsal horn of the spinal cord. It is activated by opioids, stress, and certain analgesic drugs. Key structures include the midbrain, brainstem, and descending projections via the dorsolateral funiculus.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — The periaqueductal grey (PAG) in the midbrain is the principal hub of descending inhibition. When activated by endogenous opioids or exogenous morphine, it projects to the rostral ventromedial medulla, which in turn modulates dorsal horn activity.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — The gamma efferent system refers to motor neurons controlling intrafusal fibres in muscle spindles. It is part of the motor system and plays no role in pain modulation.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — The locus coeruleus in the pons is the main noradrenergic nucleus in the brain. It contributes to descending inhibition via noradrenergic pathways acting on α2 receptors in the dorsal horn — the basis of action of drugs like clonidine and duloxetine.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — The nucleus tractus solitarius is a brainstem nucleus involved in autonomic and visceral afferent processing. It is not a primary component of the descending inhibitory pain pathway, though indirect connections exist.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — The nucleus raphe magnus is a serotonergic nucleus in the rostral ventromedial medulla. It is a key relay in the PAG–RVM–spinal cord axis, exerting inhibitory effects on nociceptive neurons in the dorsal horn via serotonergic projections.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_methadone_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":7,"stem":"Methadone:","choices":["Has a predictable half-life of 8–12 hours","Prolongs the QTc interval","Is a pure mu-opioid receptor agonist","May accumulate with repeated dosing due to redistribution from tissue stores","Requires dose reduction in renal failure"],"correct":[false,true,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>Methadone is a synthetic opioid used in pain management and opioid substitution therapy. It has unique pharmacological properties that distinguish it from other opioids, including NMDA receptor antagonism and serotonin reuptake inhibition. Its complex pharmacokinetics make dose titration hazardous.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Methadone has a highly variable and unpredictable half-life ranging from 8–120 hours, most commonly quoted as 24–36 hours. This wide variability reflects inter-individual differences in CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 metabolism and makes accumulation a significant clinical risk.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Methadone prolongs the QTc interval, predisposing to torsades de pointes, particularly at higher doses and in patients with hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, or concurrent use of other QTc-prolonging drugs. A baseline ECG is recommended before initiation.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Methadone is not a pure mu-agonist. It also acts as an NMDA receptor antagonist — contributing to efficacy in neuropathic pain and reducing opioid tolerance — and inhibits reuptake of serotonin and noradrenaline.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Methadone is highly lipophilic and accumulates in fat, muscle and other tissues. With repeated dosing, tissue stores build up and then continue to release drug into plasma — this is why late accumulation and respiratory depression can occur days after what appeared to be a stable dose.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Unlike morphine, whose active metabolite morphine-6-glucuronide accumulates in renal failure, methadone is primarily hepatically metabolised and does not require dose reduction in renal failure. This makes it a useful option in patients with chronic kidney disease requiring strong opioids.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_coeliac_plexus_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":8,"stem":"The coeliac plexus:","choices":["Lies directly anterior to the crura of the diaphragm","Lies at the level of the L1–L2 vertebrae","Lies directly anterior to the aorta","Lies directly posterior to the pancreas","Receives preganglionic fibres from the greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves"],"correct":[true,false,true,true,true],"explanation":"<p>The coeliac plexus is the largest autonomic plexus in the abdomen. It transmits nociceptive signals from upper abdominal viscera including the stomach, liver, biliary tree, pancreas, spleen, and small bowel to the splenic flexure. Coeliac plexus block and neurolysis are used for pancreatic cancer and chronic pancreatitis pain.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — The coeliac plexus lies directly anterior to the crura of the diaphragm at the level of T12–L1. This is clinically important when performing posterior approaches, where needles are directed toward this region.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — The plexus lies at the level of T12–L1, not L1–L2. Confusion between these levels is a common examination error. The aortic bifurcation is at L4; the coeliac axis originates from the aorta at T12/L1.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — The coeliac plexus surrounds the origins of the coeliac axis and superior mesenteric artery and is positioned directly anterior to the aorta between these vessels.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — The pancreas lies anterior to the coeliac plexus, which is therefore directly posterior to it. A pancreatic tumour can encase the plexus, complicating neurolytic block.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — The coeliac plexus receives preganglionic sympathetic fibres via the greater splanchnic (T5–T9), lesser splanchnic (T10–T11), and least splanchnic (T12) nerves. These carry visceral afferents centrally and efferent sympathetic signals peripherally.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_pancreatic_cancer_pain_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":9,"question":"A 68-year-old woman with known pancreatic adenocarcinoma presents with severe epigastric and back pain rated 9/10. She is on oral morphine 120 mg modified-release twice daily but pain control is inadequate and side effects of nausea and constipation are limiting dose escalation. Her performance status is declining. She has no coagulopathy. What is the single best next step in pain management?","choices":["Switch to transdermal fentanyl patch","Add pregabalin 75 mg twice daily","Perform coeliac plexus neurolysis","Commence ketamine infusion","Refer for cordotomy"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>Pancreatic cancer pain is primarily visceral nociceptive pain transmitted via the coeliac plexus. When systemic opioids are dose-limited by side effects or inadequate analgesia, coeliac plexus neurolysis using alcohol is a well-evidenced interventional option. The WHO analgesic framework supports early interventional approaches in cancer pain when systemic analgesia is insufficient.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect</strong> — Switching to transdermal fentanyl addresses the opioid side effect profile, particularly constipation, but does not tackle the root cause of inadequate analgesia. It represents an opioid rotation rather than treatment targeting the pain mechanism, and is unlikely to be sufficient given the severity of pain.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. Incorrect</strong> — Pregabalin may add modest benefit if there is a neuropathic component from nerve invasion, but the primary mechanism here is visceral nociception. It would not address the dominant pain pathway and introduces additional side effects without substantial benefit.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. Correct</strong> — Coeliac plexus neurolysis interrupts nociceptive signalling from the pancreas and upper abdomen via the splanchnic nerves. RCT evidence demonstrates superior pain control and reduced opioid consumption compared with systemic analgesia alone. It is the intervention of choice when opioids are inadequate or poorly tolerated in upper abdominal cancer pain.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. Incorrect</strong> — Ketamine infusion can be effective for opioid-refractory pain through NMDA antagonism, but requires inpatient administration and monitoring. It would not be the best single next step over a targeted intervention directly addressing the pain mechanism.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. Incorrect</strong> — Cordotomy interrupts the spinothalamic tract and is reserved for unilateral somatic or neuropathic cancer pain in patients with short life expectancy. It is not the first-line interventional choice for bilateral visceral pancreatic pain and carries risks of weakness, bladder dysfunction, and Ondine''s curse with bilateral procedures.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_cabg_brachial_plexus_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":10,"question":"A 65-year-old man who underwent a coronary artery bypass graft six months ago presents with inability to extend the ring and little fingers of his right hand. He reports numbness and pain along the medial aspect of the forearm and the ulnar two fingers. On examination there is wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles and a claw-hand deformity of the ring and little fingers. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["C8 cervical radiculopathy","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Ulnar nerve injury at the elbow","Median nerve injury at the wrist","Brachial plexus lower trunk injury"],"correct":4,"explanation":"<p>Brachial plexus injuries following cardiac surgery typically affect the lower trunk (C8–T1), which is vulnerable to stretch during sternal retraction or positioning during prolonged procedures. The lower trunk supplies both the intrinsic hand muscles via the ulnar nerve and the medial forearm skin via the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm — a combination that localises the lesion proximal to the elbow.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect</strong> — C8 radiculopathy produces a similar finger pattern and sensory loss in the same dermatomal territory. However, it does not explain the medial forearm sensory loss (which is not a C8 dermatome) and the perioperative temporal relationship to prolonged cardiac surgery points strongly away from a cervical cause.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. Incorrect</strong> — Thoracic outlet syndrome can affect the lower trunk and produce a similar distribution, but typically presents in younger patients, often female, with positional symptoms provoked by arm elevation. It is not classically precipitated by sternotomy and sternal retraction.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. Incorrect</strong> — Ulnar nerve injury at the elbow produces ring and little finger weakness and intrinsic wasting. However, it spares the medial forearm, as the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm arises from the medial cord proximal to the elbow. Medial forearm sensory loss localises the lesion to the lower trunk or medial cord, not an isolated ulnar neuropathy at the elbow.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. Incorrect</strong> — Median nerve injury at the wrist produces weakness of the LOAF muscles and sensory loss over the lateral 3½ fingers. It does not explain ring/little finger weakness or medial forearm sensory loss.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. Correct</strong> — The lower trunk (C8–T1) supplies intrinsic hand muscles via the ulnar nerve and medial forearm sensation via the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm. Prolonged CABG with sternal retraction is a well-recognised cause of lower trunk brachial plexopathy. The combination of intrinsic wasting, ring/little finger clawing, and medial forearm sensory loss following cardiac surgery is the classic presentation.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_opioid_renal_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":11,"question":"A 72-year-old woman with metastatic ovarian cancer and stage 4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 18 ml/min) requires strong opioid analgesia for poorly controlled pain. She is currently on oral morphine 30 mg modified-release twice daily with inadequate analgesia and is becoming increasingly drowsy and confused. Which opioid is the most appropriate substitution to minimise toxic accumulation?","choices":["Oxycodone","Tramadol","Hydromorphone","Fentanyl","Codeine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p>In renal failure, opioids that rely on renal excretion of active or neuroexcitatory metabolites are hazardous. Morphine, oxycodone, hydromorphone, and codeine all have metabolites that accumulate in renal impairment. The drowsiness and confusion in this patient are consistent with morphine-6-glucuronide accumulation. Fentanyl is primarily hepatically metabolised to the inactive metabolite norfentanyl and is the safest strong opioid in significant renal impairment.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect</strong> — Oxycodone is metabolised to oxymorphone (active) and noroxycodone. While oxymorphone accumulation is less problematic than morphine-6-glucuronide, oxycodone is not recommended as first-choice in severe renal failure and is inferior to fentanyl in this setting.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. Incorrect</strong> — Tramadol should be avoided when eGFR is below 30. Its active metabolite O-desmethyltramadol accumulates, it lowers the seizure threshold, and it risks serotonin syndrome through its noradrenaline and serotonin reuptake inhibition. It is also insufficient as a strong analgesic for cancer pain.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. Incorrect</strong> — Hydromorphone produces hydromorphone-3-glucuronide, which accumulates in renal failure and is neuroexcitatory, causing myoclonus, cognitive impairment, and seizures. It is not a safe alternative in severe renal impairment.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. Correct</strong> — Fentanyl undergoes extensive hepatic metabolism to norfentanyl, which is pharmacologically inactive and does not accumulate in renal failure in clinically significant amounts. It can be delivered transdermally, making it practical in this patient and the preferred strong opioid in significant renal impairment.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. Incorrect</strong> — Codeine is a prodrug converted by CYP2D6 to morphine, whose active metabolites accumulate in renal failure. It is contraindicated in renal impairment, and even in normal renal function it does not provide adequate analgesia for cancer pain at this stage.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_opioid_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":12,"stem":"Opioid pharmacology and clinical scenarios","lead":"For each scenario below, select the single most appropriate opioid from the options above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Buprenorphine","Codeine","Fentanyl","Hydromorphone","Methadone","Pethidine","Remifentanil","Tapentadol"],"questions":[{"question":"A 45-year-old man is undergoing surgery under general anaesthesia. The anaesthetist requires an opioid with an ultra-short, predictable duration of action that is independent of hepatic or renal function for intraoperative analgesia.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. Remifentanil</strong> — Remifentanil has a unique ester bond in its structure that is hydrolysed by non-specific plasma and tissue esterases, giving it an elimination half-life of approximately 3–5 minutes. This is entirely independent of hepatic or renal function. Its context-sensitive half-time remains short regardless of infusion duration, making it the ideal opioid for infusion-based intraoperative analgesia when rapid offset is required."},{"question":"A 58-year-old woman with chronic cancer pain has a prolonged QTc interval of 490 ms on her pre-treatment ECG. She requires strong opioid analgesia. Which of the available strong opioids has the least risk of QTc prolongation?","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Buprenorphine</strong> — Of the strong opioids, buprenorphine has the least effect on QTc prolongation at standard analgesic doses. Methadone is the opioid most strongly associated with QTc prolongation and torsades de pointes. In a patient with an already prolonged QTc, buprenorphine — available as a transdermal patch or sublingual formulation — is the preferred strong opioid with a favourable cardiac safety profile."},{"question":"A 32-year-old woman in acute severe pain following a renal colic episode is given an intramuscular opioid in the emergency department. She subsequently develops a generalised tonic-clonic seizure. Which opioid is most likely responsible?","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. Pethidine</strong> — Pethidine is metabolised to norpethidine, a neuroexcitatory metabolite that accumulates even with standard doses and lowers the seizure threshold. This is a well-known toxicity that has led to pethidine being largely removed from formularies in many hospitals. The risk is amplified in renal impairment and with repeated dosing."},{"question":"A 70-year-old man with severe COPD and end-stage renal failure (eGFR 8 ml/min) requires a strong opioid patch for chronic cancer pain. Which opioid is most appropriate?","correct":2,"explanation":"<strong>C. Fentanyl</strong> — Fentanyl is metabolised to the inactive metabolite norfentanyl and does not accumulate in renal failure. It is available as a transdermal patch, which is appropriate for patients with COPD where respiratory sensitivity makes dose titration important and where a non-oral route is advantageous. Its predictable transdermal kinetics make it suitable for this patient."},{"question":"A 40-year-old man on opioid substitution therapy presents for management of acute postoperative pain. He is on a daily supervised oral opioid programme. This opioid is also known to act as an NMDA receptor antagonist.","correct":4,"explanation":"<strong>E. Methadone</strong> — Methadone is used in opioid substitution programmes as a once-daily supervised oral dose. In addition to mu-opioid agonism, it is an NMDA receptor antagonist — contributing to neuropathic pain efficacy and reducing analgesic tolerance — and inhibits serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake. This multimodal pharmacological profile is unique among opioids and a frequently examined characteristic."}]}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_statistics_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":13,"stem":"Regarding statistical concepts used in pain medicine research:","choices":["The number needed to treat (NNT) is the inverse of the absolute risk reduction","A normal distribution has the mean equal to the median","In a positively skewed distribution, the mean is less than the median","The Mann-Whitney U test is a non-parametric equivalent of the independent samples t-test","Increasing sample size reduces the risk of a type II error"],"correct":[true,true,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Statistical literacy is directly examined in the FFPMRCA. Key concepts include measures of central tendency and spread, statistical test selection for parametric versus non-parametric data, and clinical epidemiology metrics such as NNT and NNH.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — NNT = 1 / ARR (absolute risk reduction). If a treatment reduces the risk of an event from 40% to 30%, the ARR is 10% and the NNT is 10. Lower NNT values indicate more effective treatment. This formula is fundamental and frequently tested.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — In a perfect normal (Gaussian) distribution, the mean, median, and mode are all identical, coinciding at the centre of the symmetrical bell curve. This is a defining characteristic of the normal distribution.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — In a positively skewed (right-skewed) distribution, there is a long tail to the right caused by high-value outliers. These outliers pull the mean upward relative to the median, so mean > median in positive skew. A helpful mnemonic: the mean is dragged in the direction of the skew.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — The Mann-Whitney U test is used to compare two independent groups when data are non-parametric. It is the non-parametric equivalent of the independent samples t-test. For paired non-parametric data, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test is used instead.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — A type II error is failing to detect a true effect (false negative). The power of a study (1 – β) increases with sample size. A larger sample provides greater ability to detect a true difference, which is why underpowered studies fail to reach statistical significance even when a real effect exists.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_psychology_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-06-08","rotationIndex":14,"stem":"Regarding psychological models and assessment in chronic pain:","choices":["Fear-avoidance behaviour is associated with poorer outcomes in chronic low back pain","Catastrophising is defined as the tendency to overestimate the threat value of pain","The Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (HADS) was specifically designed for use in pain clinics","Operant conditioning in the context of pain refers to the modification of pain behaviour by its consequences","The McGill Pain Questionnaire assesses only the intensity of pain"],"correct":[true,true,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>Psychological factors are central to the biopsychosocial model of chronic pain. The FFPMRCA curriculum specifically includes fear-avoidance, catastrophising, psychological assessment tools, and conditioning models, which appear in both MTF and SBA format.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — The fear-avoidance model (Vlaeyen &amp; Linton) proposes that pain leads to catastrophic misinterpretation, fear of movement (kinesiophobia), avoidance behaviour, and disability in susceptible patients. This cycle is strongly associated with poor rehabilitation outcomes in chronic musculoskeletal pain and is a cornerstone of cognitive-behavioural pain management programmes.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Pain catastrophising involves three components: magnification (exaggerating pain threat), rumination (persistent focus on pain), and helplessness (belief that nothing can be done). The Pain Catastrophising Scale (PCS) by Sullivan measures it quantitatively. High scores predict poorer treatment outcomes and greater disability.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — The HADS was developed by Zigmond and Snaith in 1983 for use in general medical and surgical outpatient settings, not specifically for pain clinics. It was designed to detect anxiety and depression while avoiding somatic items such as sleep disturbance and fatigue that could be confounded by physical illness. It is widely used in pain clinics but was not purpose-built for them.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Operant conditioning (Fordyce model) proposes that pain behaviours — guarding, grimacing, help-seeking, and medication use — can be positively or negatively reinforced by environmental responses such as attention or avoidance of responsibility. This is the theoretical basis for operant-behavioural treatment in pain management programmes, which aim to reduce reinforcement of pain behaviour and increase well behaviour.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — The McGill Pain Questionnaire (MPQ), developed by Melzack and Torgerson, is a multidimensional tool assessing sensory (e.g. throbbing, stabbing), affective (e.g. exhausting, fearful), and evaluative dimensions of pain using word descriptor categories. The VAS and NRS measure intensity only; the MPQ is a far richer instrument.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_radiation_units_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-15","rotationIndex":15,"stem":"Regarding units and measurement of ionising radiation:","choices":["The gray (Gy) is the SI unit of absorbed dose, defined as one joule of energy absorbed per kilogram of tissue","The sievert (Sv) expresses absorbed dose to tissue without any adjustment for the type of radiation involved","The becquerel (Bq) is a measure of radioactivity, defined as one nuclear disintegration per second","Effective dose takes into account the differing radiosensitivity of the various organs and tissues exposed","The roentgen is the current SI unit used to quantify a patient''s ionising radiation exposure in clinical practice"],"correct":[true,false,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. True.</strong> The Gray is defined exactly this way (J/kg) and measures absorbed dose only, with no biological weighting.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. False.</strong> The Sievert is used for equivalent and effective dose, which are derived from absorbed dose by applying weighting factors for radiation type and tissue sensitivity — it is not a raw, unadjusted measure.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. True.</strong> This is the standard definition of the Becquerel, replacing the older Curie.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> This is precisely what distinguishes effective dose from equivalent dose — it sums weighted equivalent doses across all exposed organs according to their relative radiosensitivity.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. False.</strong> The roentgen is an older, non-SI unit of exposure (ionisation in air). The SI unit of exposure is the coulomb per kilogram (C/kg); clinical dose reporting now uses Gray and Sievert rather than the roentgen.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Radiation Dose Quantities</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><p style=\"margin:0\">When ionising radiation interacts with the body, several distinct quantities are used to describe it, and confusing them is a common source of error. Absorbed dose (Gray) simply measures energy deposited per unit mass, regardless of the biological effect that energy produces. To account for the fact that different types of radiation (alpha, beta, X-ray, neutron) cause different amounts of biological damage for the same absorbed dose, a radiation weighting factor is applied to give the equivalent dose, measured in Sieverts. X-rays have a weighting factor of 1, so 1 gray = 1 Sievert. A further tissue weighting factor (reflecting how radiosensitive a given organ is) gives the effective dose — also expressed in Sieverts. Activity, the rate at which a radioactive source decays, is a separate concept measured in Becquerels and is unrelated to the dose received by a person nearby.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_radiation_dose_ct_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-15","rotationIndex":16,"question":"A 68-year-old man under investigation for back pain has, over the preceding year, undergone a chest X-ray, an isotope bone scan, a CT scan of the head, and a fluoroscopically-guided lumbar transforaminal epidural injection. He is now referred for a CT of the abdomen and pelvis. Of the following, the investigation delivering the highest effective radiation dose to this patient is:","choices":["Chest X-ray","CT abdomen and pelvis","CT head","Fluoroscopically-guided lumbar transforaminal epidural injection","Isotope bone scan"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Chest X-ray — incorrect.</strong> The lowest-dose investigation listed; a single view of a well-collimated, low-attenuation area.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. CT abdomen and pelvis — correct.</strong> Large-volume CT of a region containing radiosensitive organs gives the highest effective dose among these options.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. CT head — incorrect.</strong> Lower dose than CT abdomen/pelvis, as the volume scanned is smaller and contains less radiosensitive tissue.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. Fluoroscopic lumbar epidural — incorrect.</strong> Dose is generally lower than CT, being dependent on screening time, and modern pulsed fluoroscopy and collimation keep this comparatively low.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. Isotope bone scan — incorrect.</strong> Delivers a moderate whole-skeleton dose from the radiopharmaceutical, but this remains lower than a large-volume CT study.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_radiation_dose_xray_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-15","rotationIndex":17,"question":"A patient attending the pain clinic mentions that over the past year they have had several plain X-ray examinations performed for unrelated reasons: a zygomatic view, a skull series, a chest film, a thoracic spine series, and a lumbar spine series. Of the following, which examination is likely to have delivered the highest effective radiation dose?","choices":["Zygoma X-ray","Skull X-ray","Chest X-ray","Thoracic spine X-ray","Lumbar spine X-ray"],"correct":4,"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Zygoma — incorrect.</strong> A small facial bone view requiring minimal exposure factors and involving very little radiosensitive tissue; among the lowest-dose examinations listed.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. Skull — incorrect.</strong> Slightly higher dose than the zygoma view due to larger area and bone thickness, but still a relatively low-dose examination, as no highly radiosensitive organs lie directly in the beam.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. Chest — incorrect.</strong> Despite imaging a large area, the lung''s air content means much of the beam passes through with low attenuation, keeping the effective dose low.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. Thoracic spine — incorrect.</strong> Requires higher exposure factors than the chest or skull due to greater bone density and soft tissue depth, but the dose remains lower than for the lumbar spine.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. Lumbar spine — correct.</strong> This examination requires the highest exposure factors of those listed, owing to the greater depth of tissue to penetrate, and the beam passes close to the gonads, pelvic organs, and active bone marrow — all radiosensitive tissues — making this the highest-dose plain film examination among the options given.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_let_ionising_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":18,"stem":"Linear energy transfer (LET) of ionizing radiation is likely to:","choices":["Be measured in kiloelectronvolts (keV) per micrometre (µm) of medium","Be high with X-rays","Be low with alpha particles","Cause effects by direct action when it is at a high level","Lead to the generation of destructive free radicals when it is at a high level"],"correct":[true,false,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. True.</strong> LET is expressed as kiloelectronvolts (keV) of energy deposited per micrometre (µm) of medium traversed. It quantifies how densely a type of radiation deposits energy along its path — a higher LET means more energy deposited per unit distance.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. False.</strong> X-rays are a low-LET form of radiation. They interact sparsely with tissue, producing widely spaced ionisation events along their path. Gamma rays are similarly low-LET.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. False.</strong> Alpha particles are a high-LET form of radiation. Their large mass and double positive charge cause dense, closely-spaced ionisation events over a very short path length — conferring high biological effectiveness despite limited tissue penetration.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> High-LET radiation acts predominantly by direct action: it directly ionises critical molecules such as DNA, triggering destructive chemical or physical reactions without requiring an intermediary.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. False.</strong> Free radical generation is the mechanism of low-LET radiation, not high-LET radiation. Low-LET radiation (X-rays, gamma rays) acts mainly by indirect action — ionising water molecules to produce reactive hydroxyl radicals (·OH) that then damage DNA. This indirect mechanism accounts for approximately two-thirds of DNA damage from low-LET radiation.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_radiation_cell_vulnerability_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":19,"stem":"When exposed to ionizing radiation:","choices":["Nerve cells are more vulnerable than haemopoietic cells","Gut cells are less vulnerable than haemopoietic cells","Gastrointestinal symptoms are immediate when the dose is 0.5 Gy","Death occurs in 100% of cases within 2 weeks after exposure to 10–20 Gy","Death occurs due to central nervous system failure after exposure to at least 20 Gy"],"correct":[false,true,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. False.</strong> The cells most vulnerable to radiation are those with the greatest rate of replication and least differentiation — the Law of Bergonié and Tribondeau. Haemopoietic precursors divide rapidly and are highly radiosensitive, whereas nerve cells are post-mitotic and among the least radiosensitive tissues in the body.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. True.</strong> Gut epithelial cells do divide actively, but haemopoietic precursors divide even more rapidly and are more susceptible to radiation-induced apoptosis. The hierarchy from most to least radiosensitive: haemopoietic → reproductive → gastrointestinal → epidermis/eyes → other organs → support tissues → nerve.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. False.</strong> Immediate gastrointestinal symptoms appear at doses above 1.5 Gy, not at 0.5 Gy. At 0.5 Gy, only blood count changes are expected. Nausea, vomiting, and fatigue are likely at 0.5–1.5 Gy but become immediate only once the dose exceeds 1.5 Gy.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> Whole-body exposure to 10–20 Gy is uniformly fatal within two weeks. The cause of death is gastrointestinal tract failure — ablation of the rapidly dividing intestinal epithelium leads to mucosal sloughing, fluid loss, bacterial translocation, and overwhelming sepsis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. True.</strong> Exposure to more than 20 Gy causes death due to central nervous system failure. Despite nerves being comparatively radioresistant, extreme doses cause overwhelming vascular injury, cerebral oedema, and neuronal death, typically within hours to a few days.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Whole-Body Dose &amp; Radiosensitivity</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><thead><tr><th>Absorbed Dose (Gy)</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&lt; 0.05</td><td>No immediate effect</td></tr><tr><td>0.05–0.5</td><td>Change in blood count</td></tr><tr><td>0.5–1.5</td><td>Change in blood count; likely nausea, vomiting, fatigue, and loss of appetite</td></tr><tr><td>1.5–10</td><td>GI symptoms appear immediately; severe changes in blood; 1.5 Gy is the death threshold (most sensitive individuals); 3–5 Gy — 50% die within 60 days without intensive support; death due to bone marrow suppression, requiring transplant to recover</td></tr><tr><td>10–20</td><td>Death in 100% within 2 weeks; death due to GI failure</td></tr><tr><td>&gt; 20</td><td>Death due to CNS failure</td></tr></tbody></table><img src=\"images/radiosensitivity-pyramid.png\" alt=\"Radiosensitivity pyramid diagram — Bergonié and Tribondeau law showing tissue radiosensitivity from highest (haematopoietic) to lowest (nerve), correlated with rate of cell division\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:16px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_fluoroscopy_dose_reduction_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":20,"stem":"Radiation exposure during fluoroscopy is likely to be reduced by:","choices":["Beam collimation","Continuous fluoroscopy rather than pulsed fluoroscopy","Magnification","Use of a laser cross to guide imaging","Moving the body part for imaging closer to the image intensifier"],"correct":[true,false,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. True.</strong> Beam collimation limits the divergence of X-rays, restricting the radiation field to only the area of interest. This improves image quality without increasing dose, and reduces scatter radiation to staff.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. False.</strong> Continuous fluoroscopy increases the total radiation dose compared with pulsed fluoroscopy. Pulsed techniques use intermittent exposures at a reduced frame rate, substantially cutting the dose rate. Switching from continuous to pulsed fluoroscopy is a key strategy for dose reduction.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. False.</strong> Magnification requires a higher dose of ionising radiation to maintain image quality, because the detector captures a smaller field area, necessitating increased X-ray output. Using the lowest appropriate magnification factor minimises dose.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> The laser cross (or cross-hair targeting system) allows the operator to align and target the image intensifier accurately before initiating any exposure. By ensuring correct positioning before the fluoroscopy run, unnecessary screening time is avoided.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. True.</strong> Moving the body part closer to the image intensifier reduces beam divergence, improving image quality with less radiation. Greater source-to-skin distance and shorter object-to-image intensifier distance both reduce dose to the patient.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_radiation_cancer_risk_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":21,"stem":"The lifetime risk of cancer is likely to be:","choices":["One in a million after chest X-ray","Greater after skull X-ray than after chest X-ray","Lower after abdominal X-ray than skull X-ray","Increased in children who are exposed to ionizing radiation in utero","Lower after chest computed tomography than after chest X-ray"],"correct":[true,true,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. True.</strong> A chest X-ray delivers approximately 0.02 mSv of effective radiation dose — equivalent to about 3 days of background radiation. This corresponds to a lifetime cancer risk of approximately 1 in 1,000,000, making it among the lowest-risk radiological investigations.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. True.</strong> A skull X-ray delivers a higher effective dose than a chest X-ray (approximately 0.06–0.1 mSv versus 0.02 mSv), corresponding to a lifetime cancer risk of approximately 1 in 300,000 compared with 1 in 1,000,000 for a chest X-ray.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. False.</strong> The lifetime cancer risk from an abdominal X-ray is <em>greater</em> than from a skull X-ray — approximately 1 in 30,000 versus 1 in 300,000. This is because the abdomen contains highly radiosensitive organs (gonads, bowel, active marrow) within the primary beam.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> Exposure to ionising radiation in utero during organogenesis and the fetal stage (approximately 4–40 weeks) causes genetic mutations that increase the lifetime risk of cancer in the child. The developing fetus is particularly radiosensitive due to rapid cell division throughout organogenesis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. False.</strong> Chest CT delivers a substantially higher effective dose than a chest X-ray — approximately 5–8 mSv versus 0.02 mSv — corresponding to a lifetime cancer risk of approximately 1 in 2,500 compared with 1 in 1,000,000. CT is the highest-dose of the common diagnostic imaging techniques.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_irmer_justification_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":22,"question":"Under the Ionising Radiation (Medical Exposure) Regulations (IRMER), who holds legal responsibility for justifying a fluoro-guided medical exposure before it goes ahead?","choices":["Radiation Protection Supervisor","Referrer","Operator","Practitioner","Employer"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> The Radiation Protection Supervisor (RPS) is a role defined under the Ionising Radiation Regulations (IRR), not IRMER. The RPS oversees day-to-day compliance with staff and public radiation safety in designated areas — this is an occupational safety role, not a patient-exposure role.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> The referrer requests the exposure and provides the clinical information that justifies the need for the investigation. However, the referrer does not hold the legal responsibility for authorising the individual exposure — that duty is assigned specifically to the practitioner.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> The operator is whoever practically carries out any part of the exposure — for example, pressing the fluoroscopy pedal, positioning the patient, or administering contrast. Operators must be adequately trained but do not bear the legal responsibility for justification.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. Correct.</strong> Under IRMER, the <strong>practitioner</strong> is the person legally accountable for justifying that a specific medical exposure is appropriate for the individual patient before it is carried out. The practitioner must be entitled to act in this role under their employer''s written procedures.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> The employer''s duty under IRMER is to establish the systems, protocols, and training that enable compliance across the organisation — for example, drawing up written procedures and ensuring adequate equipment. The employer does not justify individual patient exposures.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">IRMER vs IRR</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><p style=\"margin:0\">IRMER (Ionising Radiation [Medical Exposure] Regulations) and the Ionising Radiation Regulations (IRR) are frequently confused because they both concern ionising radiation, but they protect different people and define different duties. IRR protects staff and the public from occupational and environmental radiation exposure: it covers designating controlled and supervised areas, appointing a Radiation Protection Adviser (RPA) and Radiation Protection Supervisor (RPS), and setting dose limits for workers. IRMER, by contrast, protects the patient undergoing a medical exposure and defines four key duty-holder roles: the <strong>referrer</strong> (supplies the clinical justification for requesting the exposure), the <strong>practitioner</strong> (legally responsible for justifying each individual exposure), the <strong>operator</strong> (practically carries out the exposure), and the <strong>employer</strong> (establishes the organisational framework of procedures and training).</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_absorbed_dose_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":23,"stem":"Whole-body absorbed radiation dose","lead":"For each of the following clinical scenarios, select the single most likely whole-body absorbed radiation dose from the list of options above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["<0.05 Gy","0.05–0.5 Gy","0.5–1.5 Gy","1.5–10 Gy","10–20 Gy","≥20 Gy","≥50 Gy","Dose is not the primary determinant of outcome"],"questions":[{"question":"A radiographer''s routine personal dosimetry badge detects an exposure during a monthly check. The radiographer reports no nausea, no blood count changes, and no symptoms at any point during the month.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. &lt;0.05 Gy.</strong> The complete absence of any clinical effect — including blood count changes — places this exposure firmly below 0.05 Gy. At this level, only dosimetry can detect the exposure. Radiographers working under regulated conditions with appropriate shielding and distance will typically accumulate doses far below the annual occupational dose limit of 20 mSv."},{"question":"A worker accidentally exposed during a source-handling incident develops mild transient nausea and a modest fall in lymphocyte count over the following days. The worker makes a full recovery within a week without any specific treatment.","correct":2,"explanation":"<strong>C. 0.5–1.5 Gy.</strong> Mild nausea and a modest lymphopenia with spontaneous recovery without specific treatment is characteristic of this dose range. Blood count changes begin at around 0.05 Gy; nausea and fatigue become likely at 0.5 Gy. Full spontaneous recovery suggests the dose did not reach the 1.5 Gy threshold where severe haemopoietic failure requiring intervention would be expected."},{"question":"A worker exposed during an industrial accident develops confusion, ataxia, and seizures within hours, followed by cardiovascular collapse and death within 24–48 hours.","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. ≥20 Gy.</strong> Neurological symptoms (confusion, ataxia, seizures) appearing within hours, followed by death within 24–48 hours, is the hallmark of CNS syndrome — caused only by extreme whole-body doses above 20 Gy. At these doses, the comparatively radioresistant central nervous system sustains overwhelming vascular and neuronal injury. This presentation is rapid and invariably fatal; no treatment can meaningfully delay death."},{"question":"A patient exposed during a radiological incident develops vomiting within hours, followed by pancytopenia requiring transfusion support and prophylactic antibiotics. Intensive supportive care, including consideration of stem cell rescue, is given and the patient survives.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. 1.5–10 Gy.</strong> Vomiting within hours indicates a dose above 1.5 Gy. Progressive pancytopenia requiring transfusion and antibiotic support, with consideration of stem cell transplantation, confirms significant haemopoietic syndrome. Survival with intensive support — including possible bone marrow rescue — is consistent with a dose in the 1.5–10 Gy range. At 3–5 Gy, 50% of individuals will die within 60 days without such support; doses towards the top of this range make transplant essential."},{"question":"Following a major radiation incident, a patient develops profuse bloody diarrhoea with evidence of gastrointestinal mucosal sloughing and overwhelming sepsis. Despite maximal supportive treatment in intensive care, the patient dies within two weeks.","correct":4,"explanation":"<strong>E. 10–20 Gy.</strong> Bloody diarrhoea with GI mucosal sloughing, overwhelming sepsis, and death within two weeks despite maximal care is the signature presentation of GI syndrome. At 10–20 Gy, the intestinal epithelium is ablated faster than it can regenerate, causing mucosal barrier failure, bacterial translocation, fluid loss, and invariably fatal sepsis. Death in 100% of cases within two weeks is the expected outcome at this dose, regardless of treatment."}],"explanation":"<p>The severity and timing of acute radiation syndrome (ARS) depend critically on the whole-body absorbed dose and follow a predictable pattern governed by the radiosensitivity of different tissues. Per the Law of Bergonié and Tribondeau, rapidly dividing, poorly differentiated cells are most radiosensitive. At very low doses, only dosimetry detects the exposure. As dose rises, the haemopoietic system — the most radiosensitive of the major organ systems — is affected first, producing a prodromal illness that may resolve fully. At higher doses, haemopoietic syndrome becomes life-threatening, requiring transfusion or transplant. Higher still, the gastrointestinal epithelium fails faster than it can be replaced, producing GI syndrome with near-certain mortality within two weeks. At extreme doses exceeding 20 Gy, even the comparatively radioresistant CNS sustains fatal injury within hours to a few days.</p><table style=\"margin-top:20px\"><thead><tr><th>Absorbed Dose (Gy)</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&lt; 0.05</td><td>No immediate effect</td></tr><tr><td>0.05–0.5</td><td>Change in blood count</td></tr><tr><td>0.5–1.5</td><td>Change in blood count; likely nausea, vomiting, fatigue, and loss of appetite</td></tr><tr><td>1.5–10</td><td>GI symptoms appear immediately; severe changes in blood; 1.5 Gy is the death threshold (most sensitive individuals); 3–5 Gy — 50% die within 60 days without intensive support; death due to bone marrow suppression, requiring transplant to recover</td></tr><tr><td>10–20</td><td>Death in 100% within 2 weeks; death due to GI failure</td></tr><tr><td>&gt; 20</td><td>Death due to CNS failure</td></tr></tbody></table>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_radiation_distance_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":24,"choices":["Wearing a thyroid shield","Wearing lead-lined gloves","Wearing lead-impregnated eye protection","Increasing distance from the radiation source","Wearing a standard lead apron"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> A thyroid shield is effective but protects only the thyroid, which is a relatively small radiosensitive target; it does nothing to reduce whole-body dose.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Lead-lined gloves are useful specifically when the operator''s hands must be near or in the beam (which should itself be avoided where possible), but they do not address whole-body exposure and are a targeted, situational measure rather than a global one.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Lead-impregnated eyewear protects the lens of the eye from cataract risk but, like the other targeted PPE options, provides no reduction in whole-body dose.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. Correct.</strong> Governed by the inverse square law, increasing distance from the radiation source is the single most powerful and universally applicable method of reducing occupational dose, since it reduces exposure to the whole body — not just a shielded area. Doubling the distance reduces dose to one quarter.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> A standard lead apron reduces dose to the trunk from scatter radiation but does not protect the head, neck, or extremities, and is less impactful than simply increasing distance from the source.</p>","question":"Of the following, which measure provides the greatest reduction in occupational radiation dose to the operator?"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_fluoroscopy_measures_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":25,"stem":"Regarding practical measures to reduce radiation exposure during fluoroscopically-guided procedures:","choices":["Beam collimation reduces the area of tissue irradiated and reduces scatter to the operator","Continuous fluoroscopy delivers a lower cumulative dose than pulsed fluoroscopy for an equivalent screening time","Positioning the image intensifier as close as possible to the patient reduces the dose required to produce an adequate image","Digital magnification imaging increases the dose delivered to the patient compared with standard field-of-view imaging","Standing on the same side as the X-ray tube, rather than the image intensifier, increases operator exposure to scatter radiation"],"correct":[true,false,true,false,true],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. True.</strong> Collimating the beam to the smallest field necessary for the procedure reduces the volume of tissue exposed and reduces the amount of scattered radiation produced, which in turn reduces operator dose.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. False.</strong> Pulsed fluoroscopy, not continuous fluoroscopy, reduces cumulative dose for an equivalent screening time, since the beam is only active for brief pulses rather than continuously — continuous fluoroscopy delivers a considerably higher dose.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. True.</strong> Positioning the image intensifier (receptor) as close as possible to the patient allows the same image quality to be achieved with a lower dose; conversely, increasing the air gap between patient and receptor requires higher exposure factors to compensate.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. False.</strong> Digital magnification is performed post image acquisition and so has no effect on dose. If the stem referred to optical or geometric magnification (a genuine acquisition-time change), that would be true — but digital magnification is a post-processing mode.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. True.</strong> Scatter radiation is greatest on the side of the X-ray tube (where the primary beam enters the patient), so an operator standing on the tube side receives more scatter than one standing on the image intensifier side — a good reason to position oneself, where possible, on the receptor side of the C-arm.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_peripheral_nerve_injury_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":26,"stem":"With regards to peripheral nerve injury:","choices":["Neurapraxia is characterised by disruption of the axon with preservation of the endoneurial scaffold.","Sunderland Grade III injury involves axonal and endoneurial disruption with preservation of the perineurium and epineurium.","Wallerian degeneration is typically well established within 1–2 weeks of a neurotmetic injury.","Neurapraxia is associated with Wallerian degeneration and recovery occurring over several months.","Sunderland Grade V injury is synonymous with neurotmesis, involving complete disruption of both axons and the scaffold."],"correct":[false,true,true,false,true],"explanation":"<p>A traumatic peripheral nerve injury can be graded by Seddon''s classification (neurapraxia, axonotmesis, neurotmesis) or Sunderland''s five-grade system, which predicts prognosis and the need for surgery.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – False:</strong> Neurapraxia is a myelin injury only; the axon and scaffold remain intact and Wallerian degeneration does not occur.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – True:</strong> Sunderland Grade III involves axonal and endoneurial disruption but the perineurium and epineurium remain preserved.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – True:</strong> Wallerian degeneration begins within days and is well established by 1–2 weeks after axonal injury.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – False:</strong> Neurapraxia recovers rapidly (3–6 weeks) via remyelination, without Wallerian degeneration.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – True:</strong> Grade V is complete neurotmesis — disruption of both axons and the entire scaffold.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Seddon &amp; Sunderland Classification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><div style=\"text-align:center\"><img src=\"images/seddon_sunderland_classification.png\" alt=\"Seddon-Sunderland classification table comparing nerve injury grades\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:0;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_peripheral_nerve_structure_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":27,"stem":"Regarding the cellular structure of a peripheral nerve:","choices":["The endoneurium is the outermost layer of the nerve and confers its tensile strength.","Groups of axons are organised into fascicles, each surrounded by the perineurium.","The basal lamina surrounding each axon is secreted by Schwann cells.","Schwann cells retain their repair phenotype indefinitely, regardless of any delay to reinnervation.","Peripheral nerves receive their blood supply from a single dominant vascular pedicle at each level."],"correct":[false,true,true,false,false],"explanation":"<p>Peripheral nerve structure is organised through a central nervous tissue (axon) which conducts electrical signal. This is wrapped around by myelin from glial cells which speeds signal transmission. On the outer layer of the glial cells is a layer called the basal lamina which is made of proteins. These are then surrounded by layers of connective tissue from inside to the outside as follows: endoneurium → fascicle → perineurium → epineurium.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – False:</strong> The epineurium is the outermost layer and provides tensile strength; the endoneurium surrounds individual axons.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – True:</strong> Fascicles (groups of axons) are each wrapped in the multilayered perineurium.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – True:</strong> The basal lamina is an extracellular matrix secreted by Schwann cells, maintaining axon–Schwann cell alignment.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – False:</strong> If reinnervation is delayed beyond 6–12 weeks, Schwann cells progressively lose their repair phenotype, with some undergoing apoptosis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – False:</strong> Nerves are supplied by widely separated segmental pedicles feeding longitudinal vascular plexi, not a single pedicle.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Peripheral Nerve Structure</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><div style=\"text-align:center\"><img src=\"images/axon-image.png\" alt=\"Diagram of peripheral nerve cellular structure showing axon, Schwann cell, basal lamina, endoneurium, fascicle, perineurium, and epineurium\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:0;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_pain_mechanisms_nerve_injury_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":28,"stem":"Pain mechanisms following nerve injury:","choices":["Upregulation of voltage-gated sodium channels facilitates spontaneous action potential generation at the injury site.","Central sensitisation is mediated in part by activation of AMPA and NMDA receptors on second-order dorsal horn neurones.","Descending inhibitory noradrenergic tone from the locus coeruleus is typically enhanced following nerve injury.","Neuromas contain reduced densities of voltage-gated sodium and mechanosensitive ion channels compared with normal nerve.","Aberrant sympathetic sprouting can upregulate α1-adrenoceptor expression on nociceptors, producing sympathetically maintained pain."],"correct":[true,true,false,false,true],"explanation":"<p>Peripheral nerve injury triggers a cascade of peripheral and central changes underlying neuropathic pain.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – True:</strong> Upregulated Na⁺ channels drive spontaneous ectopic firing at the site of injury.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – True:</strong> Glutamate and substance P release activates AMPA/NMDA receptors, driving wind-up and long-term potentiation.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – False:</strong> Noradrenergic descending inhibition from the locus coeruleus diminishes after injury, reducing inhibitory control.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – False:</strong> Neuromas show increased densities of voltage-gated sodium and mechanosensitive channels, causing spontaneous and mechanically evoked firing.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – True:</strong> Sympathetic sprouting can upregulate α1-adrenoceptors on nociceptors, producing sympathetically maintained pain (cold allodynia, colour change).</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_interventional_neuropathic_pain_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":29,"stem":"Interventional techniques for neuropathic pain:","choices":["Cryoneurolysis achieves permanent nerve destruction through disruption of the endoneurial scaffold.","Dorsal root ganglion stimulation is superior over spinal cord stimulation for focal neuropathic pain.","Peripheral nerve stimulation is best suited to diffuse pain involving multiple nerves.","Botulinum toxin type A exerts part of its analgesic effect through inhibition of SNAP-25-dependent neurotransmitter release.","Perineural hydrodissection using saline is reserved for cases where dextrose is contraindicated."],"correct":[false,true,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>Neuromodulation and injectate-based techniques act via distinct, targeted mechanisms.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – False:</strong> Cryoneurolysis preserves the scaffold and produces a reversible conduction block, unlike radiofrequency ablation which disrupts it.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – True:</strong> The ACCURATE trial showed DRGS had a significantly higher responder rate than SCS for focal neuropathic pain.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – False:</strong> PNS is best suited to single-nerve injury; SCS is preferred for diffuse or multi-nerve involvement.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – True:</strong> BoNT-A''s analgesic effect is mediated through SNAP-25 inhibition, sodium channel suppression, and TRPV1 modulation.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – False:</strong> Both saline and dextrose 5% in water are non-neurotoxic; saline gives inert mechanical separation while D5W may add antinociceptive effects.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_neuropathic_botox_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":30,"question":"A woman develops focal neuropathic pain over the anterolateral foot after fibular fracture fixation. Ultrasound-guided electrical stimulation of a superficial sensory nerve branch reproduces her exact pain pattern, and a targeted local anaesthetic injection gives complete but transient relief. Which of the following is the next therapeutic step?","choices":["Spinal cord stimulation","Perineural botulinum toxin type A injection proximal to the neuroma","Removal of the fibular metalwork","Oral gabapentin titration","Targeted muscle reinnervation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p><strong>A – Incorrect:</strong> SCS may be indicated if Botox therapy is not helpful, as this is a more invasive option.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B – Correct:</strong> Perineural BoNT-A proximal to the neuroma is a minimally invasive option and would be appropriate to trial in this case.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C – Incorrect:</strong> Metalwork removal is considered when irritation/compression from hardware is the primary driver, not established here.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D – Incorrect:</strong> Pharmacotherapy optimisation should already have been attempted before interventional referral; it isn''t the \"next step\" after positive diagnostic blocks.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E – Incorrect:</strong> TMR is a surgical option for non-functional nerves/terminal neuromas, not the first step after a positive diagnostic block.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_diffuse_neuropathic_neuromod_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":31,"question":"A man has diffuse neuropathic pain affecting overlapping distributions of several nerves in his lower leg after a crush injury. Which neuromodulation modality is most appropriate?","choices":["Peripheral nerve stimulation","Dorsal root ganglion stimulation","Spinal cord stimulation","Pulsed radiofrequency","Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>Choice of neuromodulation depends on whether pain is focal/single-nerve or diffuse/multi-nerve.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A – Incorrect:</strong> PNS is best suited to single-nerve injury with focal pain, not diffuse multi-nerve involvement.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B – Incorrect:</strong> DRGS suits focal dermatomal pain or where peripheral targeting is not feasible, not diffuse disease.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C – Correct:</strong> SCS is indicated for diffuse or multi-nerve involvement, and is the most established modality with NICE support.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D – Incorrect:</strong> PRF is a needle-based technique targeting a specific peripheral nerve, not suited to diffuse multi-nerve pain.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E – Incorrect:</strong> TENS has limited evidence for long-term benefit and is a first-line, non-invasive option rather than a targeted solution for refractory diffuse pain.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_hydrodissection_carpal_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":32,"question":"A woman has persistent neuropathic pain in her hand three years after carpal tunnel decompression. Ultrasound suggests tethering of the median nerve within postoperative scar tissue, and pharmacological therapy has failed. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial interventional option?","choices":["Spinal cord stimulation","Surgical neurolysis","Ultrasound-guided perineural hydrodissection","Targeted muscle reinnervation","Cryoneurolysis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>Hydrodissection is specifically indicated where scar, tethering, or fascial entrapment is suspected, and precedes more invasive options.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A – Incorrect:</strong> SCS is reserved for refractory diffuse pain after other options fail, not first-line for a suspected mechanical/tethering cause.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B – Incorrect:</strong> Surgical neurolysis is considered if less invasive measures fail or if structural pathology clearly requires surgical release.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C – Correct:</strong> US-guided hydrodissection produces ≥50% symptom relief in the majority of patients with persistent post-carpal-tunnel-surgery pain, often avoiding surgical revision.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D – Incorrect:</strong> TMR is for non-functional nerves/neuromas, not a nerve suspected to be simply tethered in scar.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E – Incorrect:</strong> Cryoneurolysis is typically applied to sensory or mixed nerves for refractory neuropathic pain, not as first-line management of suspected tethering.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_interventional_neuropathic_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":33,"stem":"Interventional techniques for neuropathic pain","lead":"For each scenario below, choose the single most appropriate intervention from the list above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation","Ultrasound-guided perineural local anaesthetic injection","Perineural botulinum toxin type A","Perineural hydrodissection","Pulsed radiofrequency","Cryoneurolysis","Peripheral nerve stimulation","Dorsal root ganglion stimulation"],"questions":[{"question":"A patient with a suspected symptomatic neuroma has pain in a region of overlapping sensory innervation from two adjacent nerves; the treating clinician needs to confirm which nerve is responsible before planning any therapeutic intervention.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Ultrasound-guided perineural local anaesthetic injection</strong> — Diagnostic US-guided local anaesthetic injection, ideally combined with nerve stimulator confirmation of symptom reproduction, establishes nerve–pain concordance before committing to therapy."},{"question":"Injection of an agent that works through inhibition of SNAP-25-dependent neurotransmitter release.","correct":2,"explanation":"<strong>C. Perineural botulinum toxin type A</strong> — BoNT-A''s analgesic mechanism involves SNAP-25 inhibition, sodium channel suppression, and TRPV1 modulation, and it responds particularly well in superficial allodynia."},{"question":"An intervention with low evidence for long-term benefit in chronic neuropathic pain.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation</strong> — TENS is inexpensive, safe, and non-invasive, making it a reasonable first-line trial, though evidence for long-term benefit in traumatic peripheral nerve injury is limited."},{"question":"An intervention that uses the Joule–Thomson effect to produce analgesia.","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. Cryoneurolysis</strong> — Cryoneurolysis uses the Joule–Thomson effect to cool tissue to approximately −70°C, inducing Wallerian-like degeneration while preserving the endoneurial scaffold — unlike radiofrequency ablation, this gives a reversible conduction block."},{"question":"An intervention to target neuropathic pain in the distribution of a single peripheral nerve.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. Peripheral nerve stimulation</strong> — PNS is delivered via a percutaneous trial lead before permanent implantation and, per the COMFORT trial, is best suited to single-nerve injury with focal neuropathic pain."}]}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_nrb_consent_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-05","rotationIndex":34,"stem":"Regarding Nerve Root Blocks:","choices":["Non-particulate steroids are licensed for use in neuraxial procedures.","Verbal consent alone is sufficient; a signed consent form is not required.","Non-particulate steroids should be preferred over particulate steroids.","The clinician providing treatment must always personally obtain consent.","Consent discussion should include information about the option of taking no action."],"correct":[false,false,true,false,true],"explanation":"<p>Consent for nerve root blocks must be adequately informed, cover the off-label use of corticosteroids and material risks, and be documented in writing before the procedure.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – False:</strong> All corticosteroid preparations — both particulate and non-particulate — are used off-label (''beyond licence'') for neuraxial procedures, as none currently holds an MHRA marketing authorisation for epidural use; patients must be consented accordingly.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – False:</strong> Written consent should be obtained for all nerve root blocks/epidural injections for pain of spinal origin, documented on a separate signed consent form kept in the patient''s records.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – True:</strong> Inadvertent intravascular injection of particulate steroid into a radicular artery can form precipitates that occlude the vessel, risking spinal cord or brainstem ischaemia — non-particulate steroids avoid this risk.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – False:</strong> The responsibility can be delegated to another suitably trained and competent clinician with sufficient knowledge of the treatment, provided GMC guidance is followed; the treating clinician should still review the patient''s decision immediately before treatment.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – True:</strong> Consent discussions must cover potential benefits and adverse outcomes, including the option of taking no action, alongside side-effects, complications, and material risks.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_nrb_contrast_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-05","rotationIndex":35,"stem":"Use of contrast in nerve root blocks:","choices":["Contrast medium used must be non-ionic and water-soluble.","Luer connectors are the recommended standard for neuraxial procedures.","Negative aspiration can safely rule out intravascular position of the needle.","Ultrasound is an acceptable alternative to fluoroscopy for performance of nerve root blocks.","Image guidance is recommended primarily to shorten procedure time."],"correct":[true,false,false,false,false],"explanation":"<p>Accurate needle placement is central to safe and effective nerve root blocks, and the guideline places significant emphasis on fluoroscopic technique, connector safety, and image retention as governance requirements.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – True:</strong> A non-ionic, water-soluble contrast medium licensed for spinal (including intrathecal) injection must be used to confirm needle placement and spread.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – False:</strong> Non-Luer (NRFit) connectors are now mandated for neuraxial procedures, following a National Patient Safety Alert aimed at preventing wrong-route errors; standard Luer connectors are no longer appropriate.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – False:</strong> Small vessels can occlude under the negative pressure of aspiration, giving a false-negative result — intravascular spread must instead be excluded by injecting contrast under live fluoroscopy.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – False:</strong> Ultrasound can help position the needle, but FPM guidance recommends fluoroscopy to confirm final needle position, with contrast used to confirm spread and exclude intravascular injection.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – False:</strong> Image guidance''s stated purpose is to provide a higher degree of precision, minimise complications, and improve reliability of outcomes — not primarily to reduce procedure time.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_nrb_monitoring_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-05","rotationIndex":36,"stem":"Monitoring for nerve root blocks:","choices":["Monitoring (ECG, NIBP, SpO2) should be continued for the duration of the procedure only.","New limb weakness after the procedure should be managed with routine same-day discharge and outpatient follow-up.","Patients should be given a contact option to report complications such as headache, fever, prolonged numbness/weakness or urinary retention.","Emergency full spine MRI scanning is a necessary resource for services providing this procedure.","Inadvertent dural puncture may necessitate a more prolonged hospital admission."],"correct":[false,false,true,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Because nerve root blocks carry a small but serious risk of neurological or infective complications, the guideline sets minimum standards for monitoring duration, discharge safety-netting, and urgent access to imaging and surgical opinion.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – False:</strong> ECG, NIBP and SpO2 monitoring should commence before the procedure and continue for 30 minutes after block completion, not stop at the end of the procedure.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – False:</strong> Significant limb weakness, sensory loss or headache on the day of the procedure may necessitate an unplanned overnight admission with review the next day before discharge, rather than routine same-day discharge.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – True:</strong> After discharge, patients should be given a contact option to report acute complications including headache, fever, prolonged numbness/weakness or urinary retention.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – True:</strong> Emergency full spine MRI scanning should be available, with arrangements in place for urgent neurosurgical or spinal surgical referral if needed.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – True:</strong> If the procedure is complicated by inadvertent dural puncture, the patient may require a more prolonged admission and management per local guidance.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_nrb_antiplatelet_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-05","rotationIndex":37,"question":"A 68-year-old man on clopidogrel for a coronary stent placed four months ago is considered for a transforaminal lumbar nerve root block for radicular pain. He asks whether he must stop his clopidogrel beforehand. What is the most appropriate next step regarding his antiplatelet therapy?","choices":["Stop clopidogrel seven days before the procedure.","Continue clopidogrel unchanged and proceed with the block as planned.","Discuss interruption of clopidogrel with the cardiologist, weighing bleeding and thrombotic risk.","Substitute clopidogrel with aspirin for the peri-procedural period.","Permanently cancel the procedure while he remains on clopidogrel."],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Unilaterally stopping clopidogrel risks stent thrombosis, particularly this soon after stenting, and bypasses the need for individualised risk assessment.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Continuing unchanged does not account for the increased bleeding/haematoma risk of neuraxial-adjacent procedures in patients on antiplatelet therapy.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> The guideline states that interruption of antiplatelet/anticoagulant therapy should be considered after evaluating bleeding and thrombotic risk, discussed with the physician or GP supervising that therapy, following current published guidelines.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Substituting aspirin is not a guideline-supported bridging strategy and does not resolve the underlying risk-benefit decision, which needs specialist input given the recent stenting.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Cancellation is not automatic — many patients on antiplatelets can still safely proceed after appropriate individualised risk assessment and discussion with the supervising physician.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_caudal_epidural_risk_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-05","rotationIndex":38,"question":"Which of the following represents an increased risk specific to the caudal route for epidural injections?","choices":["Contrast medium allergy","Skin contamination","Radiation exposure","Local anaesthetic toxicity","Vasovagal syncope"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Contrast allergy risk is not route-specific — it applies equally to any procedure requiring contrast medium.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> FPM guidance specifies that particular care is required with skin preparation for the caudal route, because of the increased risk of skin contamination in that area, given its proximity to the perineum/natal cleft.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Radiation exposure considerations apply across all fluoroscopically guided levels, not specifically to caudal skin preparation.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity relates to drug dose and inadvertent intravascular injection, and is not specific to the caudal route.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Vasovagal syncope is a recognised generic procedural complication, but is not the specific risk the guideline associates with caudal skin preparation.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_anticoag_rivaroxaban_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":39,"question":"A 58-year-old man taking rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily for atrial fibrillation, with normal renal function, is due to have a cervical transforaminal nerve root block for cervical radicular pain. What is the most appropriate management of his rivaroxaban before the procedure?","choices":["Continue rivaroxaban unchanged","Discontinue rivaroxaban 3 days before the procedure","Discontinue rivaroxaban 5 days before the procedure","Discontinue rivaroxaban 7 days before the procedure","Bridge with therapeutic-dose LMWH after stopping rivaroxaban"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p>Cervical transforaminal nerve root blocks sit in the high-risk category because of the proximity to the vertebral artery and spinal cord, so anticoagulation must be interrupted rather than continued.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Continuing a DOAC through a high-risk procedure is not acceptable.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> DOACs are renally cleared and should be stopped for 5 half-lives before high-risk procedures. For rivaroxaban with normal renal function, this equates to 3 days (the exception being severe renal impairment &mdash; CrCl &lt;15 ml/min &mdash; where clearance becomes unpredictable).</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C, D. Incorrect.</strong> These durations are excessive for a DOAC with normal renal clearance and unnecessarily prolong the patient''s time off anticoagulation. Dabigatran is more reliant on renal clearance and is therefore stopped for 4 days rather than 3 (reflecting its longer half-life) &mdash; this does not apply to rivaroxaban.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Bridging is not routinely required for DOACs. In cases of very high thrombotic risk, this should be assessed on an individual basis with the haematology team.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Procedural Risk Stratification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">\n    <tr><th>Risk Category</th><th>Example Procedures</th></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--wrong);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">High</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Spinal cord stimulation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Intrathecal drug delivery systems</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\"><td style=\"color:#7A5200;font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Intermediate</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Interlaminar epidurals</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sympathetic blocks (stellate, thoracic, splanchnic, coeliac, lumbar, hypogastric)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--correct);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Low</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral joint &amp; musculoskeletal injections</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Trigger point injections (incl. piriformis)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sacroiliac joint injection &amp; sacral lateral branch blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Pocket revision &amp; IPG placement</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--stone);margin:2px 0 16px\">In patients with elevated bleeding risk, reclassify intermediate-risk procedures as high risk and low-risk procedures as intermediate.</div>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--correct);margin-bottom:8px\">Low Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>Continue &mdash; ensure last dose taken &ge;6 hours before the procedure. Individual assessment if bleeding risk is high or INR &gt;3.0.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>Omit on day of procedure &mdash; allow &ge;2 half-lives since last dose (usually 24 hours). Longer if CrCl &lt;15 (drug accumulates unpredictably).</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>As other DOACs</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5200;margin-bottom:8px\">Intermediate Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Consider stopping if anatomically higher bleeding risk (e.g. cervical epidural, stellate ganglion block)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--wrong);margin-bottom:8px\">High Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Primary prevention: 6 days<br>Secondary prevention: 4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_anticoag_warfarin_valve_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":40,"question":"A 70-year-old woman on warfarin for a mechanical aortic valve requires an elective intrathecal drug delivery system implant. Her INR is 3.4 (target range 2.5–3.5). What is the most appropriate perioperative anticoagulation strategy?","choices":["Continue warfarin unchanged throughout","Stop warfarin with no bridging, restart afterwards","Stop warfarin, bridge with therapeutic-dose LMWH, restart warfarin post-procedure","Switch to a DOAC for the perioperative period","Reduce the warfarin dose without stopping, to lower the INR to 2.0"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>Intrathecal pump implantation is a high-risk procedure requiring an INR &le;1.2, but this patient''s mechanical valve places her in the high thromboembolic-risk category.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> An INR of 3.4 is unsafe for a high-risk neuraxial-adjacent implant.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Leaving a mechanical valve patient unprotected during the anticoagulant-free window risks valve thrombosis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> Bridging with a form of heparin is reserved for patients like this, whose thromboembolic risk without anticoagulation is high &mdash; distinct from routine AF, where the BRIDGE trial showed bridging is unhelpful. Discussion with the local team is warranted to decide whether therapeutic-dose LMWH (stopped 24 hours pre-procedure) is safe, or whether admission for IV heparin (stopped 6 hours pre-procedure) is necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Mechanical valves are not a licensed indication for DOACs, which have shown inferior outcomes in this population.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> An INR of 2.0 is still too high for a high-risk procedure and does not address the interruption/bridging question.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Procedural Risk Stratification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">\n    <tr><th>Risk Category</th><th>Example Procedures</th></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--wrong);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">High</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Spinal cord stimulation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Intrathecal drug delivery systems</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\"><td style=\"color:#7A5200;font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Intermediate</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Interlaminar epidurals</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sympathetic blocks (stellate, thoracic, splanchnic, coeliac, lumbar, hypogastric)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--correct);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Low</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral joint &amp; musculoskeletal injections</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Trigger point injections (incl. piriformis)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sacroiliac joint injection &amp; sacral lateral branch blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Pocket revision &amp; IPG placement</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--stone);margin:2px 0 16px\">In patients with elevated bleeding risk, reclassify intermediate-risk procedures as high risk and low-risk procedures as intermediate.</div>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--correct);margin-bottom:8px\">Low Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>Continue &mdash; ensure last dose taken &ge;6 hours before the procedure. Individual assessment if bleeding risk is high or INR &gt;3.0.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>Omit on day of procedure &mdash; allow &ge;2 half-lives since last dose (usually 24 hours). Longer if CrCl &lt;15 (drug accumulates unpredictably).</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>As other DOACs</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5200;margin-bottom:8px\">Intermediate Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Consider stopping if anatomically higher bleeding risk (e.g. cervical epidural, stellate ganglion block)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--wrong);margin-bottom:8px\">High Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Primary prevention: 6 days<br>Secondary prevention: 4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_anticoag_dapt_stent_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":41,"question":"A 62-year-old man had a drug-eluting coronary stent inserted 6 weeks ago and remains on dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin and clopidogrel). He requests a lumbar facet medial branch block for ongoing back pain. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Proceed with the block, continuing both antiplatelet agents","Stop clopidogrel for 7 days and proceed, continuing aspirin","Stop both agents for 7 days and proceed","Postpone the elective procedure and liaise with cardiology regarding timing","Proceed after bridging with LMWH"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p>While lumbar medial branch blocks are a low-risk procedure, the key issue here is timing and dual antiplatelet therapy rather than procedure risk alone: this patient is still within the high-risk early window after drug-eluting stent insertion, when premature cessation of dual antiplatelet therapy carries a serious risk of stent thrombosis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> While continuing a single antiplatelet is often reasonable for low-risk procedures in stable patients, this doesn''t address the more pressing issue of whether an elective procedure should proceed at 6 weeks post-stent in a patient on dual antiplatelet therapy.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B, C. Incorrect.</strong> Interrupting clopidogrel this early after a drug-eluting stent carries an unacceptable risk of stent thrombosis, a life-threatening complication.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Correct.</strong> The elective procedure should be deferred, with cardiology input on safe timing &mdash; this reflects the balance of thrombotic versus bleeding risk central to the guidance, prioritising avoidance of stent thrombosis over proceeding with an elective intervention.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Bridging is not indicated for antiplatelet agents, and doesn''t address the stent thrombosis risk of stopping clopidogrel.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Procedural Risk Stratification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">\n    <tr><th>Risk Category</th><th>Example Procedures</th></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--wrong);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">High</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Spinal cord stimulation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Intrathecal drug delivery systems</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\"><td style=\"color:#7A5200;font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Intermediate</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Interlaminar epidurals</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sympathetic blocks (stellate, thoracic, splanchnic, coeliac, lumbar, hypogastric)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--correct);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Low</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral joint &amp; musculoskeletal injections</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Trigger point injections (incl. piriformis)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sacroiliac joint injection &amp; sacral lateral branch blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Pocket revision &amp; IPG placement</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--stone);margin:2px 0 16px\">In patients with elevated bleeding risk, reclassify intermediate-risk procedures as high risk and low-risk procedures as intermediate.</div>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--correct);margin-bottom:8px\">Low Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>Continue &mdash; ensure last dose taken &ge;6 hours before the procedure. Individual assessment if bleeding risk is high or INR &gt;3.0.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>Omit on day of procedure &mdash; allow &ge;2 half-lives since last dose (usually 24 hours). Longer if CrCl &lt;15 (drug accumulates unpredictably).</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>As other DOACs</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5200;margin-bottom:8px\">Intermediate Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Consider stopping if anatomically higher bleeding risk (e.g. cervical epidural, stellate ganglion block)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--wrong);margin-bottom:8px\">High Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Primary prevention: 6 days<br>Secondary prevention: 4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_anticoag_ssri_nsaid_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":42,"question":"A 45-year-old woman taking sertraline for depression and regular ibuprofen for osteoarthritis is listed for a sacroiliac joint injection. Which statement about her bleeding risk is most accurate?","choices":["SSRIs alone carry bleeding risk and should be stopped before intervention","The combination of an SSRI and NSAID modestly increases bleeding risk and should prompt caution but not necessarily stopping pre-procedure","NSAIDs have no effect on periprocedural bleeding risk","SSRIs should be substituted with a tricyclic antidepressant before any pain procedure","Concurrent NSAID and SSRI use is an absolute contraindication to intervention"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p>SSRIs impair platelet serotonin uptake and can modestly potentiate bleeding, particularly in combination with other haemostasis-altering drugs &mdash; but this is a risk to be weighed, not an automatic trigger for stopping treatment.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> The independent bleeding risk of SSRIs alone is low; routine cessation before intervention is not supported.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> The combined effect of an SSRI and an NSAID is a recognised, modest increase in bleeding risk, warranting caution (especially in elderly or hepatically impaired patients) rather than mandatory discontinuation of either drug.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> NSAIDs do reversibly inhibit platelet cyclooxygenase and can contribute to bleeding risk, particularly for higher-risk procedures.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> There is no indication to switch antidepressant class purely for a pain procedure.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> This combination is a caution, not an absolute contraindication.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_anticoag_lmbb_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":43,"stem":"Stopping medications pre-lumbar medial branch block","lead":"For each scenario below, choose the single most appropriate management from the list above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Continue medication unchanged","Discontinue 3 days before the procedure","Discontinue 5 days before the procedure","Discontinue 7 days before the procedure","Commence bridging therapy with therapeutic-dose LMWH","Normalise the INR before proceeding","Discontinue 24 hours before the procedure","Postpone the procedure"],"questions":[{"question":"A patient taking low-dose aspirin (75 mg) for secondary cardiovascular prevention is due to have a lumbar transforaminal epidural.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Continue unchanged.</strong> Low-dose aspirin is not routinely stopped for intermediate-risk procedures; guidance supports a shared, individualised decision rather than blanket cessation."},{"question":"A patient taking apixaban for atrial fibrillation, with normal renal function, is listed for genicular blocks.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Continue unchanged.</strong> Genicular blocks are low-risk procedures in an easily compressible area, and the risk of stopping anticoagulation here outweighs that of continuing. If the patient has multiple additional bleeding-risk factors, this can be considered on an individual basis."},{"question":"A patient taking clopidogrel following a bare-metal coronary stent inserted 10 years ago is listed for a spinal cord stimulator implant.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. Discontinue 7 days before.</strong> Clopidogrel''s irreversible effect means several days are needed for platelet turnover before a high-risk procedure; a bare-metal stent from a decade ago no longer carries an acute thrombosis risk that would preclude interruption."},{"question":"A patient on long-term warfarin for a mechanical heart valve requires an interlaminar cervical epidural.","correct":4,"explanation":"<strong>E. Bridge with therapeutic-dose LMWH.</strong> A mechanical valve places this patient in the high thromboembolic-risk category, for which bridging remains appropriate around a high-risk procedure, unlike routine AF."},{"question":"A patient taking a therapeutic dose of rivaroxaban for a pulmonary embolism sustained 3 weeks ago requires a sacroiliac joint injection.","correct":7,"explanation":"<strong>H. Postpone the procedure.</strong> A pulmonary embolism only 3 weeks earlier represents an acute, high thrombotic-risk period; interrupting therapeutic anticoagulation for an elective procedure at this stage is unsafe, so the intervention should be deferred rather than the anticoagulant interrupted."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Procedural Risk Stratification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm);padding:16px 18px\">  <table style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">\n    <tr><th>Risk Category</th><th>Example Procedures</th></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--wrong);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">High</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Spinal cord stimulation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Intrathecal drug delivery systems</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\"><td style=\"color:#7A5200;font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Intermediate</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Interlaminar epidurals</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sympathetic blocks (stellate, thoracic, splanchnic, coeliac, lumbar, hypogastric)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--correct);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Low</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral joint &amp; musculoskeletal injections</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Trigger point injections (incl. piriformis)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sacroiliac joint injection &amp; sacral lateral branch blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Pocket revision &amp; IPG placement</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--stone);margin:2px 0 16px\">In patients with elevated bleeding risk, reclassify intermediate-risk procedures as high risk and low-risk procedures as intermediate.</div>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--correct);margin-bottom:8px\">Low Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>Continue &mdash; ensure last dose taken &ge;6 hours before the procedure. Individual assessment if bleeding risk is high or INR &gt;3.0.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>Omit on day of procedure &mdash; allow &ge;2 half-lives since last dose (usually 24 hours). Longer if CrCl &lt;15 (drug accumulates unpredictably).</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>As other DOACs</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5200;margin-bottom:8px\">Intermediate Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Consider stopping if anatomically higher bleeding risk (e.g. cervical epidural, stellate ganglion block)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--wrong);margin-bottom:8px\">High Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Primary prevention: 6 days<br>Secondary prevention: 4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_receptor_moa_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":44,"stem":"Receptors and mechanisms of action of pain therapies","lead":"For each scenario below, select the single best-matching answer from the list above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Mu receptor","Delta receptor","Acetylcholine receptor","Alpha-2-delta subunit","GABA receptor","Sodium channel","TRPV1 receptor","NMDA receptor"],"questions":[{"question":"Conventional TENS (high-frequency, low-intensity), used for analgesia.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Delta receptor</strong> — Conventional (high-frequency, low-intensity) TENS is largely gate-control mediated and acts predominantly through spinal delta-opioid receptor activation, distinguishing it from acupuncture-like TENS."},{"question":"Acupuncture-like TENS (low-frequency, high-intensity), used for analgesia.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Mu receptor</strong> — Low-frequency, high-intensity acupuncture-like TENS triggers release of endogenous opioids acting at mu-opioid receptors, and its effect is more reliably reversed by naloxone than that of conventional TENS."},{"question":"Gabapentin, used for neuropathic pain.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. Alpha-2-delta subunit</strong> — Despite the name, gabapentin (and pregabalin) do not act on GABA receptors. They bind the alpha-2-delta subunit of presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing calcium influx and downstream release of excitatory neurotransmitters (glutamate, substance P) in the dorsal horn."},{"question":"Lamotrigine, used for trigeminal neuralgia.","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. Sodium channel</strong> — Lamotrigine, like carbamazepine and other membrane-stabilising anticonvulsants used in trigeminal neuralgia, works primarily by blocking voltage-gated sodium channels, stabilising their inactive state and reducing ectopic neuronal firing."},{"question":"Capsaicin 8% patch (Qutenza), used for peripheral neuropathic pain.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. TRPV1 receptor</strong> — Capsaicin 8% is a TRPV1 agonist; sustained receptor activation causes calcium influx and subsequent reversible &ldquo;defunctionalisation&rdquo; of cutaneous nociceptors, producing prolonged analgesia after a single application.<p style=\"color:#7C7068;font-size:13px;margin-top:14px\">Acetylcholine receptors (C), GABA receptors (E), and NMDA receptors (H) are not the primary mechanism for any of the five scenarios and remain unused in this set.</p>"}]}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_paediatric_pain_scales_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":45,"stem":"Paediatric pain assessment scales","lead":"For each of the following scenarios, select the most likely choice from the list of options above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["CRIES scale","COMFORT scale","Paediatric Pain Profile (PPP)","Functional Disability Inventory (FDI)","FLACC scale","Faces scale","Paediatric Quality of Life Inventory (PedsQL)","Visual Analogue Scale (VAS)"],"questions":[{"question":"35-week neonate, post-inguinal hernia repair.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. CRIES scale</strong> — CRIES (Crying, Requires oxygen, Increased vital signs, Expression, Sleeplessness) is a behavioural scale validated for acute postoperative pain in neonates from around 32 weeks&rsquo; gestation, making it appropriate for this 35-week neonate."},{"question":"12-year-old with back pain and severe cognitive dysfunction.","correct":2,"explanation":"<strong>C. Paediatric Pain Profile (PPP)</strong> — The PPP is a 20-item behaviour-rating scale validated for assessing pain in children with severe to profound neurological impairment. It is designed for use in the home setting by a parent, as well as in the hospital or hospice setting by health professionals."},{"question":"6-year-old having venepuncture.","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. Faces scale</strong> — The Faces scale is a self-report tool suited to school-age children old enough to identify with facial expressions of pain, appropriate for a cooperative 6-year-old facing a brief procedural pain event.\n<div style=\"margin-top:14px;text-align:center\">\n  <img src=\"images/faces-pain-scale.jpg\" alt=\"Faces Pain Scale — Revised, showing six faces from 0 (no pain) to 5 (worst pain)\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7;background:#FFFAF4;padding:14px 10px\">\n</div>"},{"question":"3-year-old on day 1 post emergency appendicectomy.","correct":4,"explanation":"<strong>E. FLACC scale</strong> — FLACC (Face, Legs, Activity, Cry, Consolability) is a behavioural observational scale validated for infants and young children unable to reliably self-report, fitting the postoperative 3-year-old. The revised FLACC tool identifies specific indicators of pain in children with severe cognitive impairment, including spasms, self-harm behaviours, consoling actions, and breathing or verbal noises.\n<table style=\"margin-top:14px\">\n  <tr><th>Category</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Face</strong></td><td>No particular expression or smile</td><td>Occasional grimace or frown, withdrawn, disinterested</td><td>Frequent to constant frown, quivering chin, clenched jaw</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Legs</strong></td><td>Normal position or relaxed</td><td>Uneasy, restless, tense</td><td>Kicking or legs drawn up</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Activity</strong></td><td>Lying quietly, normal position, moves easily</td><td>Squirming, shifting back and forth, tense</td><td>Arched, rigid or jerking</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Cry</strong></td><td>No cry (awake or asleep)</td><td>Moans or whimpers, occasional complaint</td><td>Crying steadily, screams or sobs, frequent complaints</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Consolability</strong></td><td>Content, relaxed</td><td>Reassured by occasional touching, hugging or being talked to, distractible</td><td>Difficult to console or comfort</td></tr>\n</table>"},{"question":"9-year-old with juvenile idiopathic arthritis, being reviewed in a chronic pain clinic.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. Paediatric Quality of Life Inventory (PedsQL)</strong> — The PedsQL is a multiple-domain tool that broadly assesses function in children with chronic pain, fitting a child being followed longitudinally in a chronic pain clinic rather than being scored for acute pain intensity. Three age-banded versions exist (young child 5&ndash;7 years, child 8&ndash;12 years, teen 13&ndash;18 years), each measuring four functional subdomains &mdash; physical, emotional, social, and school &mdash; to produce a scale summary score, physical health score, and psychosocial health score. Higher scores indicate better function, and analogous carer-proxy versions allow comparison between patient- and carer-reported function."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Unselected Options — Key Definitions</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0\">\n  <tr><th>Scale</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>COMFORT</strong></td><td>Designed to assess distress in ventilated patients: 6 behavioural items (alertness, calmness, muscle tone, movement, facial tension, respiratory response) plus 2 physiological items (heart rate, mean arterial pressure). The COMFORT-B revision removed the physiological items and added crying as a behavioural item for non-ventilated children. Takes ~3 minutes; used from 0&ndash;17 years, including unconscious or sedated patients.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Functional Disability Inventory (FDI)</strong></td><td>Self/parent-report measure of the impact of pain on physical and functional activities of daily living, used mainly in chronic paediatric pain.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Visual Analogue Scale (VAS)</strong></td><td>A 10cm (100mm) line anchored &lsquo;no pain&rsquo; to &lsquo;worst pain imaginable&rsquo;; the patient marks the line, and the score is read as the distance from zero (0&ndash;10 or 0&ndash;100 scale). Requires abstract/cognitive maturity, typically usable from ~7&ndash;8 years upward.\n    <div style=\"margin-top:10px\"><img src=\"images/vas-scale.svg\" alt=\"Visual Analogue Scale — a 10cm line anchored from no pain to worst pain imaginable\" style=\"max-width:100%\"></div>\n  </td></tr>\n</table></div></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_lower_limb_nerves_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":46,"stem":"Nerves of the lower limb","lead":"Select the single most likely nerve for each scenario. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Saphenous nerve","Obturator nerve","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN)"],"questions":[{"question":"Loss of inversion and plantar flexion; lateral foot sensory loss.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Tibial nerve</strong> — The tibial nerve supplies tibialis posterior (inversion) and gastrocnemius/soleus (plantar flexion); its lateral plantar branch supplies the lateral sole, accounting for the lateral sensory loss."},{"question":"Loss of all function below the knee following hip surgery, with medial leg/foot sensation retained.","correct":4,"explanation":"<strong>E. Sciatic nerve</strong> — Sciatic nerve injury is a recognised complication of hip surgery (retraction or stretch, especially via the posterior approach). It supplies everything below the knee via its tibial and common peroneal divisions, while medial leg/foot sensation is preserved as it arises from the saphenous nerve (a femoral branch), outside the sciatic distribution."},{"question":"Loss of eversion and dorsiflexion.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Common peroneal nerve</strong> — The common peroneal nerve divides into the superficial peroneal (eversion, peroneus longus/brevis) and deep peroneal (dorsiflexion, tibialis anterior) branches; injury causes the classic foot drop."},{"question":"Loss of adduction; sensory loss over the medial thigh.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. Obturator nerve</strong> — The obturator nerve supplies the hip adductors and provides sensation over the medial thigh."},{"question":"Sensory loss over the anterolateral thigh following inguinal hernia repair, in a patient with a high BMI.","correct":7,"explanation":"<strong>H. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN)</strong> — The LFCN is a purely sensory nerve to the anterolateral thigh; meralgia paraesthetica from LFCN compression or injury is associated with high BMI and with inguinal/pelvic surgery due to its course near the inguinal ligament."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Lower Limb Nerve — Key Definitions</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0\">\n  <tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN)</strong></td><td>A purely sensory nerve to the anterolateral thigh; meralgia paraesthetica from LFCN compression or injury is associated with high BMI and with inguinal/pelvic surgery due to its course near the inguinal ligament.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Sural nerve</strong></td><td>Purely sensory (tibial + common peroneal contributions), supplying the posterolateral leg and lateral foot/little toe; no motor component, often used as a nerve graft donor.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Femoral nerve</strong></td><td>Motor to iliacus, sartorius, pectineus and quadriceps femoris; sensory to the anterior thigh, with its saphenous branch extending to the medial leg/foot.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Saphenous nerve</strong></td><td>Purely sensory terminal branch of the femoral nerve, supplying the medial leg and medial foot to the medial malleolus/first metatarsal region; no motor function.</td></tr>\n</table></div></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_opioid_addiction_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":47,"stem":"Opioid addiction and related phenomena","lead":"Select the single most likely term for each scenario. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Addiction","Pseudoaddiction","Drug diversion","Opioid-induced hyperalgesia (OIH)","Tolerance","Generalised anxiety disorder","Opioid toxicity","Dependence"],"questions":[{"question":"A chronic pain patient describes that opioids help with pain relief, but says he also enjoys the &ldquo;high&rdquo;, and reports snorting and injecting his tablets.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Addiction</strong> — Addiction is a behavioural disorder characterised by the &ldquo;4 Cs&rdquo;: craving, compulsive use, loss of control, and continued use despite harm. Enjoyment of the psychoactive &ldquo;high&rdquo; and use via non-prescribed routes (snorting, injecting) are hallmark features distinguishing this from therapeutic opioid use."},{"question":"An elderly patient on long-term opioids presents with myoclonus, irritability, and diarrhoea.","correct":7,"explanation":"<strong>H. Dependence</strong> — Myoclonus, irritability, and diarrhoea together represent a withdrawal picture — the physical manifestation of dependence, a physiological state of neuroadaptation that produces tolerance and, if the drug is abruptly stopped or reduced, physical withdrawal symptoms."},{"question":"A patient admitted with an L3 compression fracture has her opioid changed from modified-release morphine 30mg BD to immediate-release morphine 20mg 3-hourly. She keeps requesting analgesia frequently and becomes agitated when morphine is found in her cabinet.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Pseudoaddiction</strong> — Pseudoaddiction describes drug-seeking behaviour (frequent requests, agitation, distress) arising from undertreated pain rather than true addiction; it resolves once analgesia is adequately titrated, unlike genuine addiction."},{"question":"A patient with chronic widespread pain reports that her pain worsens after taking opioids.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. Opioid-induced hyperalgesia (OIH)</strong> — OIH is a paradoxical state in which opioid exposure sensitises pain pathways, producing worsening and more widespread pain despite (or because of) increasing opioid dose — the opposite of an analgesic response."},{"question":"A patient with multiple sclerosis has escalating opioid requests to her GP, who notices that her husband — her main carer — has undergone a change in behaviour.","correct":2,"explanation":"<strong>C. Drug diversion</strong> — Drug diversion refers to a third party (here, the carer) redirecting the patient&rsquo;s prescribed opioids for their own use or sale; a carer&rsquo;s behavioural change alongside the patient&rsquo;s rising analgesia requirements should raise this suspicion."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Opioid Phenomena — Key Definitions</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0\">\n  <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Drug diversion</strong></td><td>Drug diversion refers to a third party (here, the carer) redirecting the patient&rsquo;s prescribed opioids for their own use or sale; a carer&rsquo;s behavioural change alongside the patient&rsquo;s rising analgesia requirements should raise this suspicion.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Tolerance</strong></td><td>A pharmacological phenomenon where increasing doses are required to achieve the same analgesic effect over time, due to receptor-level neuroadaptation; expected with chronic opioid use and not itself evidence of addiction.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Generalised anxiety disorder</strong></td><td>Persistent, excessive worry about multiple life domains for &ge;6 months with somatic symptoms (restlessness, muscle tension, sleep disturbance); distinct from opioid-related phenomena, though it can coexist with and complicate chronic pain management.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Opioid toxicity</strong></td><td>Accumulation of opioid/active metabolites (e.g. in renal impairment) causing sedation, respiratory depression, pinpoint pupils and myoclonus; unlike withdrawal, it typically causes constipation rather than diarrhoea, with progressive CNS/respiratory depression.</td></tr>\n</table></div></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_emq_migraine_subtypes_001', 'EMQ', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":48,"stem":"Migraine subtypes","lead":"Select the single most likely diagnosis for each scenario. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Hemiplegic migraine","Migraine with aura","Migraine without aura","Vestibular migraine","Brainstem migraine","Chronic migraine","Medication overuse headache","Tension-type headache"],"questions":[{"question":"A 28-year-old woman has recurrent headaches preceded by 20 minutes of visual zigzag lines and scotoma, followed by a throbbing unilateral headache with photophobia lasting several hours.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Migraine with aura</strong> — Migraine with aura is characterised by fully reversible visual, sensory, or speech symptoms (here, a typical visual aura of zigzag lines/scotoma) preceding or accompanying the headache."},{"question":"A 34-year-old man has episodic vertigo lasting hours, associated with headache and nausea; no vestibular signs are found between episodes, and MRI is normal.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. Vestibular migraine</strong> — Vestibular migraine presents with episodic vertigo, often with associated headache/nausea, in a patient with a migraine history; interictal vestibular examination and imaging are typically normal."},{"question":"A 45-year-old woman using triptans and codeine on most days for headache now describes near-daily headache that has become less responsive to her usual medications.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. Medication overuse headache</strong> — Defined as headache that developed or worsened while taking, for 3 months or more: triptans, opioids, ergots or combination analgesic medications on 10 or more days per month; or paracetamol, aspirin or an NSAID (alone or in combination) on 15 or more days per month."},{"question":"A 22-year-old woman has an episode of unilateral limb weakness lasting an hour, followed by headache; her mother had similar episodes.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Hemiplegic migraine</strong> — Hemiplegic migraine is a rare subtype of migraine with aura featuring motor weakness as part of the aura, often with a family history given its association with autosomal dominant genetic mutations (e.g. CACNA1A)."},{"question":"A 50-year-old woman has had headache on 20 days per month for the past 4 months.","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. Chronic migraine</strong> — Chronic migraine is defined as headache on &ge;15 days per month for more than 3 months, with features of migraine present on at least some of those days."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Migraine — Background & Diagnostic Criteria</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px\"><p style=\"margin:0\">Migraine is a primary headache disorder characterised by recurrent attacks of moderate-to-severe headache, typically unilateral and pulsating, associated with autonomic and sensory features (nausea, photophobia, phonophobia), and in some patients preceded or accompanied by transient, fully reversible neurological symptoms known as aura. It is thought to arise from a combination of neuronal hyperexcitability (cortical spreading depression, particularly relevant to aura) and activation of the trigeminovascular system, leading to release of vasoactive neuropeptides (e.g. CGRP) and central sensitisation.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Episodic vs Chronic Migraine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Episodic migraine</strong></td><td>Headache occurring on fewer than 15 days per month</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chronic migraine</strong></td><td>Headache occurring on &ge;15 days per month for &gt;3 months, with features of migraine</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Diagnostic Criteria &mdash; Migraine Without Aura</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Component</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Attack frequency</strong></td><td>At least 5 attacks fulfilling all criteria below</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Duration</strong></td><td>4&ndash;72 hours (untreated or unsuccessfully treated)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Headache features (&ge;2 required)</strong></td><td>Unilateral; pulsating quality; moderate to severe intensity; exacerbated by routine physical activity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Associated features (&ge;1 required)</strong></td><td>Nausea and/or vomiting; photophobia or phonophobia</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 4px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Diagnostic Criteria &mdash; Migraine With Aura</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Component</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Attack frequency</strong></td><td>At least 2 attacks associated with aura symptoms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aura symptom type (&ge;1 required)</strong></td><td>Visual; sensory; speech or language; motor; brainstem; retinal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aura characteristics (&ge;3 required)</strong></td><td>Gradual spread over &ge;5 min for at least one symptom; stepwise, successive onset of aura symptoms; each symptom resolves within 60 min; at least one unilateral symptom; at least one positive symptom; headache occurs during aura, or within 60 min of aura onset</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_piriformis_facet_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":49,"question":"A 46-year-old office worker presents with a 4-month history of right-sided low back pain radiating into the buttock, worse on sitting and rising from sitting. Examination reveals a weakly positive piriformis test; sacroiliac joint provocation testing is negative. Lumbar spine MRI is normal. She wants to engage with physiotherapy, but reports pain is limiting her participation. What is the most appropriate next step to facilitate physiotherapy?","choices":["Caudal epidural steroid injection","Sacroiliac joint injection","Diagnostic medial branch block","Piriformis injection","Oral steroid course"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>This vignette describes a patient in whom SIJ and lumbar discogenic/radicular causes have been reasonably excluded (negative provocation testing, normal MRI). While the piriformis test (FAIR test) is weakly positive, this is not highly diagnostically accurate. Mechanical, unilateral pain with a radicular flavour that does not extend beyond the knee should prompt exclusion of facet joint pathology via a diagnostic, local-anaesthetic-only medial branch block.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Caudal epidural steroid injection has low diagnostic value and provides no future strategy for potential radiofrequency ablation; it is better suited to lumbosacral radicular pain or foraminal stenosis.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> SIJ provocation testing is explicitly negative here, making SIJ-mediated pain unlikely and this option not the best next step.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> Diagnostic medial branch block targets the lumbar facet joints and provides excellent diagnostic value for suitability of radiofrequency denervation and long-lasting analgesia. Facet joint pain is classically mechanical (worse on extension, lateral rotation, and change of position) and can frequently refer to other areas through mechanical irritation of nearby roots via facet joint hypertrophy, or chemical irritation from inflammation of bony structures.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Piriformis syndrome — a form of &ldquo;deep gluteal syndrome&rdquo; caused by sciatic nerve irritation as it passes through or near the piriformis — is a recognised but controversial cause of buttock pain. However, the FAIR test&rsquo;s weak positivity and low diagnostic accuracy make a piriformis injection premature before facet pathology has been excluded.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Systemic steroids are not first-line for localised musculoskeletal pain and lack the targeted precision of an image-guided injection directed at the suspected pain generator.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">RF Denervation &amp; the FAIR Test</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm);\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px\">\n    <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">NICE Guidance &mdash; Radiofrequency (RF) Denervation</div>\n    <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th>Component</th><th>Requirement</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Indication for referral</strong></td><td>Non-surgical treatment has not worked, <strong>and</strong> the main source of pain is thought to come from structures supplied by the medial branch nerve, <strong>and</strong> the patient has moderate or severe localised back pain (&ge;5 on a VAS or equivalent) at the time of referral</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Prerequisite</strong></td><td>Only perform RF denervation after a <strong>positive response to a diagnostic medial branch block</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Imaging</strong></td><td>Do <strong>not</strong> offer imaging for specific facet joint pain as a prerequisite for RF denervation</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"padding:16px 18px 4px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong)\">\n    <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--stone);margin-bottom:8px\">FAIR Test (Flexion, Adduction, Internal Rotation)</div>\n    <p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">A provocation test for piriformis syndrome. The patient lies supine (or lateral, hip being tested uppermost); the hip is passively flexed to ~60&ndash;90&deg;, adducted across the midline, and internally rotated, stretching/compressing the piriformis against the sciatic nerve. A positive test reproduces the patient&rsquo;s typical buttock pain, sometimes radiating down the posterior thigh.</p>\n  </div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_gout_diagnosis_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":50,"question":"A 58-year-old man with a history of alcohol excess, ischaemic heart disease, and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents shortly after hospital discharge with an acutely painful lump over his right great toe. On examination, the lump is chalky-white in colour, irregular in shape, hot, firm, and exquisitely tender. There is no history of trauma. What is the most appropriate investigation to confirm the diagnosis?","choices":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)","Skin biopsy","Synovial fluid aspiration with polarised light microscopy","Serum uric acid level","Toe X-ray"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>This vignette describes a gouty tophus with likely acute-on-chronic gout, in a patient with classic risk factors (alcohol excess, metabolic syndrome/T2DM, cardiovascular disease, and recent physiological stress from hospital admission). The chalky, irregular, firm lump is characteristic of a tophus. While NICE guidance suggests measuring urate levels to support diagnosis, this question asks for the <strong>best diagnostic modality</strong> — which is synovial fluid aspiration demonstrating monosodium urate crystals.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> ESR is a nonspecific inflammatory marker that may be raised in acute gout (or many other inflammatory/infective conditions), but does not confirm the diagnosis or distinguish gout from other causes such as septic arthritis or pseudogout.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Skin biopsy could histologically demonstrate urate crystal deposition within a tophus, but this is more invasive, slower, and less specific/practical than joint aspiration; it is not the standard first-line diagnostic test.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> The gold-standard investigation for gout is aspiration of joint/tophus fluid with polarised light microscopy, demonstrating needle-shaped, negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals. This also allows exclusion of septic arthritis via Gram stain and culture — a crucial differential given the hot, firm, tender presentation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> NICE NG219 suggests measuring serum urate to confirm the diagnosis, and this would be the correct answer if the question asked for the best <em>next step</em> rather than the best diagnostic <em>modality</em>, which is synovial fluid analysis.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Toe X-ray may show established changes of chronic tophaceous gout (juxta-articular erosions with overhanging edges) but is neither sensitive nor specific for an acute presentation and does not provide a definitive diagnosis.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng219'',''NICE NG219 — Gout'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NICE NG219 &mdash; Gout Summary\n</button></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_gout_flare_treatment_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":51,"question":"A 55-year-old man presents with an acute flare of pain affecting his first metatarsophalangeal joint. He has no history of renal impairment, peptic ulcer disease, or cardiovascular contraindications. He has no known drug allergies. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute flare?","choices":["Paracetamol","Opioid analgesia","Etoricoxib","Topical NSAID gel","Intra-articular steroid injection"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>Acute gout flares are driven by an intense inflammatory response to monosodium urate crystal deposition. First-line management centres on rapid, effective anti-inflammatory treatment — NSAIDs (including COX-2 selective agents), colchicine, or a short course of oral corticosteroids — chosen according to comorbidities and contraindications.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Paracetamol is a simple analgesic with no significant anti-inflammatory action; it is inadequate as monotherapy for the intense inflammatory pain of an acute gout flare.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Opioid analgesia addresses pain but does nothing to treat the underlying crystal-induced inflammation, and carries unnecessary dependence/side-effect risk when effective anti-inflammatory options exist and are not contraindicated.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> Etoricoxib is a COX-2 selective NSAID specifically licensed for acute gout, providing effective anti-inflammatory analgesia. In a patient without renal, cardiovascular, or GI contraindications, an oral NSAID/COX-2 inhibitor is considered first-line treatment.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Topical NSAID gel provides insufficient systemic anti-inflammatory effect for an acute, intensely inflamed joint; it is better suited to superficial musculoskeletal pain than true crystal arthropathy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Intra-articular steroid injection is effective, particularly for monoarticular flares or when oral options are contraindicated, but requires exclusion of septic arthritis first and is generally reserved for cases where oral therapy is unsuitable or the diagnosis is already confirmed — making it not the best first-line choice over an oral NSAID in this straightforward scenario.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng219'',''NICE NG219 — Gout'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NICE NG219 &mdash; Gout Summary\n</button></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_paeds_psychology_pars_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":52,"question":"A 12-year-old boy, previously treated for Langerhans cell histiocytosis, presents with an ongoing history of low back pain. MRI demonstrates a possible pars interarticularis defect at L4/L5, without associated spondylolisthesis. His pain has become increasingly disabling, and he has started refusing to attend school. There are no red flag features (no neurological deficit, no night pain, no systemic symptoms of relapse). What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Referral to a complex spine multidisciplinary team (MDT)","Referral to a clinical psychologist","Trial of stronger analgesia (opioid escalation)","Referral for a spinal surgical opinion regarding the pars defect","Referral to physiotherapy"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p>This vignette illustrates a mismatch between a relatively minor structural finding (an isolated pars defect without slip) and a significant functional impact (school refusal) — a classic marker that psychosocial factors, rather than the structural lesion alone, are driving disability. In a child with a prior cancer diagnosis, school avoidance disproportionate to imaging findings should prompt psychological assessment as the priority, since untreated psychological/behavioural drivers (health anxiety, fear-avoidance, family anxiety around symptoms after a cancer history) will undermine any purely physical treatment approach.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> A complex spine MDT referral would be appropriate if the pars defect were felt to be a significant structural driver requiring surgical/bracing consideration (e.g. with spondylolisthesis, neurological signs, or refractory mechanical pain), but the absence of slip or red flags makes a spine-focused referral too narrow given the dominant functional/psychosocial picture.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> Given the degree of functional impairment (school refusal) out of proportion to the structural finding, and the psychologically vulnerable context of prior cancer treatment, referral to psychology is the most appropriate next step to address the primary driver of disability.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Escalating opioids in paediatric musculoskeletal pain without addressing functional/psychological drivers is inappropriate, risks medicalising a functional pain state, and carries dependence/misuse risk with limited benefit.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> A pars defect without spondylolisthesis is generally managed conservatively; surgical referral is not indicated in the absence of instability or neurological compromise.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Physiotherapy is a reasonable component of overall management, but treating the physical symptom alone without addressing the marked functional/psychological impairment (school refusal) is unlikely to resolve the presentation, and is not the best next step compared with targeting the dominant issue directly.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_paeds_mucositis_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":53,"question":"A 3-year-old girl with acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) develops severe oral mucositis following chemotherapy. What is the most appropriate treatment for her pain?","choices":["Oral morphine","Rectal ketorolac","Topical lidocaine mouthwash","IV nurse-controlled analgesia (NCA)","Bolus of intravenous opioid"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p>Severe mucositis in a young child causes intense, continuous pain with an inability to tolerate oral intake (due to mucosal breakdown and swallowing difficulty). In this age group, formal patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) is not feasible, making nurse-controlled analgesia (NCA) — where a trained nurse administers bolus doses of IV opioid according to a preset protocol, based on behavioural pain assessment — the appropriate route and mode of delivery for reliable, titratable, and rapid analgesia.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Oral intake is likely to be poorly tolerated or unreliable given severe mucosal breakdown and pain on swallowing, making the oral route unsuitable and absorption unpredictable.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> NSAIDs are relatively contraindicated in this setting due to the risk of chemotherapy-associated thrombocytopenia and bleeding from friable, ulcerated mucosa; ketorolac in particular carries a significant bleeding-risk profile.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Topical lidocaine mouthwash may provide some adjunctive symptomatic relief but is inadequate as sole treatment for severe, diffuse mucositis pain, and carries a risk of systemic absorption/toxicity with extensive mucosal ulceration, plus a risk of aspiration if oral sensation is significantly numbed in a young child.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Correct.</strong> IV nurse-controlled analgesia allows titratable, rapid opioid delivery with nurse oversight, appropriate for a 3-year-old who cannot manage a self-administered PCA device, while allowing careful monitoring for sedation/respiratory depression.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> A bolus of intravenous opioid may be a suitable initial therapy, but it is not the <em>most</em> appropriate treatment, which would be ongoing analgesia via nurse-led NCA rather than a single bolus.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Oral Mucositis</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm);\"><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(237,227,215,.6);font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span>Oral mucositis (OM) is a common side effect of chemotherapy or radiotherapy used for treating head and neck cancer, or before bone marrow transplantation.</span></div><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(237,227,215,.6);font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span>Symptoms usually start 5&ndash;10 days after chemotherapy, or 14 days after radiotherapy, and include dryness, halitosis, pain, inflammation and oral mucosal ulceration.</span></div><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(237,227,215,.6);font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span>Chemotherapy-associated OM can resolve within a few days of completing chemotherapy, but radiotherapy-associated OM can last for weeks.</span></div><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(237,227,215,.6);font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span>OM can affect nutritional status (sometimes requiring enteral or parenteral nutrition) and quality of life, and can increase hospital stay. It can also require interruptions or dose reductions in chemotherapy or radiotherapy.</span></div><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span><strong>Current treatments:</strong> comprehensive oral hygiene, good hydration, a bland soft diet, and avoiding alcohol and tobacco may increase comfort. Ice, water-based moisturisers, painkillers and NSAIDs can help reduce symptoms. Drugs such as palifermin are sometimes used to prevent or treat OM, and antibiotics may be needed for infectious complications. Low-level laser therapy may also be used to prevent or treat OM.</span></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('qmtkllqpx7nv', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":54,"question":"What is the most common type of diabetic neuropathy?","choices":["Lumbar plexopathy","Peripheral Neuropathy","Acute sensory neuropathy","Autonomic neuropathy","Focal Neuropathy"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p>Diabetic neuropathy encompasses several distinct clinical patterns, but distal symmetrical sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy is by far the most common, affecting a large proportion of long-standing diabetic patients. It typically presents with a length-dependent, symmetrical &ldquo;glove and stocking&rdquo; distribution of sensory loss and/or neuropathic pain, beginning distally in the feet and progressing proximally, as described in this vignette.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Lumbar plexopathy refers to diabetic amyotrophy (lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy), a much rarer, typically asymmetric condition causing proximal lower limb weakness and pain, usually in older patients with type 2 diabetes &mdash; not the most common pattern.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> Distal symmetrical peripheral neuropathy is the most prevalent form of diabetic neuropathy, driven by chronic hyperglycaemia-induced microvascular and metabolic nerve damage, matching this patient&rsquo;s symmetrical, distally predominant presentation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Acute sensory neuropathy is a rare, distinct subtype (sometimes termed &ldquo;insulin neuritis&rdquo; when triggered by rapid glycaemic changes) presenting with acute-onset severe burning pain and allodynia, often with minimal examination findings; it is uncommon and typically self-limiting, unlike the gradual, chronic picture described here.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Autonomic neuropathy is a recognised and important complication of diabetes (affecting cardiovascular, gastrointestinal, genitourinary, and sudomotor function) but is less common as an isolated presentation and is not the single most prevalent neuropathy subtype overall.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Focal neuropathy includes mononeuropathies (e.g. cranial nerve III palsy, carpal tunnel syndrome) &mdash; these are less common than the diffuse distal symmetrical pattern and typically present acutely/subacutely with a localised deficit rather than the diffuse picture described.</p>\n\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Classification of Diabetic Neuropathy</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><img src=\"images/diabetic-neuropathy-classification.svg\" alt=\"Classification of diabetic neuropathy — generalized symmetrical polyneuropathies and focal/multifocal neuropathies\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block\"></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_morphine_breastfeeding_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":70,"question":"A 29-year-old woman who is exclusively breastfeeding her infant is recovering from surgery and requires ongoing analgesia. The team is considering the safety of various analgesic options during lactation. Which of the following analgesics carries the greatest concern regarding safety in breastfeeding?","choices":["Morphine","Paracetamol","Ibuprofen","Bupivacaine","Clonidine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<p>Analgesic choice in breastfeeding mothers must balance maternal pain control against the risk of drug transfer into breast milk and subsequent neonatal exposure, particularly for agents with active metabolites, sedative effects, or accumulation risk in the still-immature neonatal system.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Correct.</strong> Morphine (and opioids generally) is the analgesic of greatest concern in breastfeeding. While it can be given for breastfeeding women, caution is advised as morphine and its active metabolite (morphine-6-glucuronide) pass into breast milk, and neonates — particularly those under 1 month old — have reduced clearance capacity, increasing the risk of accumulation causing sedation and respiratory depression. Repeated or high-dose maternal opioid use warrants caution and neonatal monitoring; this risk is even more pronounced with codeine in ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolisers, but morphine itself still carries meaningful concern, especially in a very young infant.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Paracetamol is considered safe in breastfeeding; only minimal amounts are transferred into breast milk, and it lacks significant sedative or respiratory depressant effects on the infant.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Ibuprofen is considered the NSAID of choice in breastfeeding due to very low milk transfer, a short half-life, and no significant active metabolites of concern.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Bupivacaine is highly protein-bound with minimal systemic absorption and negligible transfer into breast milk when used regionally (e.g. wound catheter, epidural, nerve block); considered safe in breastfeeding.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Clonidine can pass into breast milk, and there is some caution advised, but it is generally considered compatible with breastfeeding at low maternal doses with monitoring; it carries substantially less concern than systemic opioids in this context.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Pharmacology Knowledge Summary</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Morphine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Opioid agonist (mu-receptor)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic via glucuronidation → active metabolites morphine-6-glucuronide (analgesic, can accumulate in renal impairment) and morphine-3-glucuronide (neuroexcitatory, non-analgesic)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Present in breast milk in clinically relevant amounts; relative infant dose is low-moderate but neonatal clearance is immature, especially &lt;1 month</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Neonatal sedation and respiratory depression, particularly with repeated dosing or in preterm/very young infants</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Practical note</strong></td><td>Short-course, lowest effective dose preferred; monitor infant for sedation, poor feeding, reduced tone</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Paracetamol</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Non-opioid analgesic/antipyretic; exact mechanism incompletely understood — central COX-3 inhibition. May act on the endocannabinoid system via the metabolite AM404, which inhibits reuptake of anandamide, and via descending serotonergic pathways</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Rapid absorption; bioavailability 65–85%; protein binding 25%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic glucuronidation/sulfation; toxic metabolite NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione (relevant in overdose)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Minimal; considered first-line analgesic in breastfeeding</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Essentially none at therapeutic doses; standard hepatotoxicity risk in maternal overdose only</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Ibuprofen</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Non-selective NSAID (COX-1/COX-2 inhibition)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Highly protein-bound, short half-life (~2 hours)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Very low (&lt;1% of maternal dose); preferred NSAID in lactation by most authorities</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Standard NSAID risks apply to the mother (GI, renal, cardiovascular); negligible infant exposure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Caution</strong></td><td>Avoid in infants with (or at risk of) reduced renal function; general NSAID caveats around platelet function/bleeding apply to the mother</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Bupivacaine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Amide local anaesthetic (sodium channel blocker)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Highly protein-bound (~95%); hepatic metabolism via amide hydrolysis (hepatic amidases)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Minimal systemic absorption with regional techniques (wound catheters, epidural, peripheral nerve blocks); negligible milk transfer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Primarily systemic toxicity (cardiotoxicity/CNS toxicity) at high plasma levels from the maternal procedure itself, not from breastfeeding exposure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Practical note</strong></td><td>Considered compatible with breastfeeding when used as intended for regional analgesia</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clonidine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Centrally acting alpha-2 adrenergic agonist</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Uses</strong></td><td>Analgesic adjunct, sedation, and autonomic symptom control (e.g. opioid withdrawal, ADHD)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milk transfer</strong></td><td>Detectable in breast milk; some case reports of neonatal sedation/hypotension with higher maternal doses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key risk</strong></td><td>Sedation, hypotension, bradycardia in the infant, though generally regarded as compatible with breastfeeding at low doses with monitoring</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Practical note</strong></td><td>Caution advised with maternal doses higher than typical low-dose adjunct use; monitor infant alertness/feeding</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_paeds_crps_school_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":55,"question":"A 12-year-old girl presents 5 months after a well-healed tibial fracture with ongoing signs of complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS) in the affected leg. She has not attended school for 4 months. Her parents report they actively encourage her to rest and avoid school. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Involve a paediatrician in her ongoing multidisciplinary care","Make a referral to social services","Tell the parents that what they are doing constitutes abuse","Refer to the GP to make a referral to social services","Perform a lumbar sympathectomy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<p>This vignette describes a well-recognised pattern in paediatric CRPS: parental reinforcement of rest and avoidance behaviour, though well-intentioned, perpetuates disability, disuse, and functional impairment. This is a psychosocial/behavioural maintaining factor rather than a safeguarding concern or a structural/sympathetically-mediated problem requiring an invasive procedure. Paediatric CRPS management is fundamentally multidisciplinary and function-focused — physiotherapy (graded exposure/desensitisation), psychology, and family/school reintegration — and formal paediatrician involvement is a key, appropriate step to coordinate and oversee this holistic rehabilitative approach given the significant functional impact (4 months out of school).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Correct.</strong> Paediatric CRPS with major functional impact warrants coordinated paediatric involvement to drive a multidisciplinary rehabilitation plan (physiotherapy, psychology, pain team, school liaison), addressing both the CRPS itself and the family’s maintaining beliefs/behaviours — the most appropriate and proportionate next step among those listed.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Not indicated — there is no evidence of neglect, abuse, or safeguarding risk; the parents’ actions stem from misguided protectiveness, not malicious harm, making this disproportionate and likely to damage family engagement.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Inaccurate and confrontational; labelling well-intentioned but misguided behaviour as \"abuse\" is unsupported by the vignette and risks alienating the family from future treatment.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Shares the same flaw as option B — a safeguarding pathway is not indicated here, regardless of the referral route.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> An invasive, largely historical intervention with limited evidence base, not indicated as first-line (or even standard) management for paediatric CRPS, and particularly inappropriate before conservative multidisciplinary rehabilitation has been tried; sympathetic blocks/procedures are reserved for select refractory adult cases and are not first-line in children.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_transforaminal_esi_l5_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":56,"question":"A 45-year-old man presents with a 6-week history of severe right leg pain radiating from the buttock to the lateral calf and dorsum of the foot. MRI confirms a right-sided L4/5 disc prolapse causing L5 nerve root compression. What is the most effective next treatment?","choices":["Facet joint injection","Interlaminar epidural steroid injection","Transforaminal epidural steroid injection","Caudal epidural steroid injection","Sciatic nerve block"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>Lumbar radicular pain from a disc prolapse compressing a specific nerve root is best treated with an image-guided injection technique that delivers steroid/local anaesthetic as close as possible to the site of pathology — the affected nerve root within or adjacent to the neural foramen — for maximum efficacy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Facet joint injection targets facet-mediated (axial, non-radicular) back pain arising from the zygapophyseal joints; it does not address nerve root compression from a disc prolapse and is not indicated for radicular leg pain.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Interlaminar epidural steroid injection delivers steroid into the epidural space more diffusely (typically midline, in the posterior epidural space at or near the level of pathology) rather than targeting the specific compressed nerve root directly, making it less precise and generally considered less effective than a targeted transforaminal approach for single-level radiculopathy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> Transforaminal epidural steroid injection delivers medication directly to the target nerve root (here, L5) at the site of compression/inflammation via the intervertebral foramen, giving it the highest evidence base and greatest efficacy for single-level, unilateral radicular pain from disc prolapse compared to other epidural approaches. It also serves as a good clinical confirmation of pathology site if surgery is being planned.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Caudal epidural steroid injection delivers medication via the sacral hiatus, resulting in a more diffuse, lower-concentration spread that may not reliably reach a higher lumbar level (L4/5) in sufficient concentration, making it less targeted/effective than a transforaminal approach for this specific pathology.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Sciatic nerve block acts peripherally, distal to the level of pathology; this would produce diagnostically confusing widespread motor/sensory blockade of the entire nerve distribution and does not address the underlying nerve root compression/inflammation, making it inappropriate as a treatment for radiculopathy.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_opioid_renal_copd_fentanyl_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":57,"question":"A patient with severe COPD and renal failure requires opioid analgesia. Which drug is least likely to accumulate in this setting?","choices":["Hydromorphone","Fentanyl","Morphine","Oxycodone","Tramadol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p>Opioid selection in renal failure depends heavily on the drug’s metabolic pathway and whether it produces active or toxic metabolites that rely on renal clearance. In a patient with both renal failure and severe COPD, the ideal agent should avoid accumulation (renal safety) while also carrying the lowest risk of exacerbating respiratory depression.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Hydromorphone is hepatically metabolised, but its metabolite hydromorphone-3-glucuronide is renally excreted and can accumulate in renal failure, potentially causing neuroexcitatory effects (myoclonus, seizures); caution is needed with dose reduction.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> Fentanyl is metabolised hepatically via CYP3A4 to largely inactive metabolites with no clinically significant renally-cleared active metabolites, making it the opioid least likely to accumulate in renal failure. It is generally regarded as one of the safer opioids in renal impairment, though respiratory depression risk from COPD still requires careful titration regardless of drug choice.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Morphine is extensively metabolised to morphine-6-glucuronide (active, analgesic/respiratory depressant) and morphine-3-glucuronide (neuroexcitatory); both accumulate significantly in renal failure, making morphine one of the least suitable opioids in this context.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Oxycodone is safer than morphine as it is mainly hepatically metabolised to noroxycodone (inactive), but it has a minor active metabolite (oxymorphone) that can accumulate in renal impairment.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Tramadol is renally excreted along with its active metabolite (O-desmethyltramadol), which can accumulate in renal failure; it additionally lowers seizure threshold and carries serotonergic risk, making it a poor choice in this context, particularly alongside other CNS-active drugs.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Opioid Metabolism in Renal Impairment</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Renal safety ranking (most to least favourable):</strong><br>Fentanyl &gt; Oxycodone &asymp; Hydromorphone &gt; Tramadol &gt; Morphine</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Morphine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via glucuronidation (UGT2B7) — no CYP450 involvement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolites</strong></td><td>Morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G) — active, potent analgesic and respiratory depressant; morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G) — inactive at opioid receptors but neuroexcitatory (myoclonus, hyperalgesia, seizures)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Both metabolites renally cleared</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Renal failure implication</strong></td><td>Significant accumulation of both M6G and M3G → prolonged sedation, respiratory depression, and neuroexcitatory toxicity; avoid or use with major dose reduction/extended intervals</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Hydromorphone</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via glucuronidation (UGT) — no clinically significant CYP450 involvement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolites</strong></td><td>Hydromorphone-3-glucuronide (H3G) — inactive analgesically but neuroexcitatory, similar to M3G</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renally cleared</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Renal failure implication</strong></td><td>H3G accumulates, risking neuroexcitatory effects (agitation, myoclonus, seizures); caution and dose reduction needed, though generally considered somewhat more favourable than morphine</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Fentanyl</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via CYP3A4 → N-dealkylation to norfentanyl</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolites</strong></td><td>Norfentanyl — inactive</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Metabolites renally excreted but are inactive, so accumulation does not cause clinical effect</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Renal failure implication</strong></td><td>No clinically significant active metabolite accumulation; considered one of the safer opioids in renal impairment; caution still needed regarding accumulation of the parent drug with prolonged infusions (context-sensitive half-time) and with hepatic impairment</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Oxycodone</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via CYP3A4 (major, → noroxycodone, largely inactive) and CYP2D6 (minor, → oxymorphone, active/more potent)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolites</strong></td><td>Noroxycodone (weakly active), oxymorphone (active)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renally cleared</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Renal failure implication</strong></td><td>Parent drug and metabolites can accumulate, prolonging effect; requires dose reduction and caution, though clinically often better tolerated than morphine — CYP2D6 polymorphisms also affect oxymorphone formation and interpatient variability</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Tramadol</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via CYP2D6 → O-desmethyltramadol (M1, active metabolite with high mu-affinity, largely responsible for opioid analgesic effect) and CYP3A4 pathways</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Additional mechanism</strong></td><td>Also inhibits serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake (non-opioid analgesic contribution)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Both parent drug and M1 metabolite renally excreted</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Renal failure implication</strong></td><td>Accumulation of both tramadol and active M1 metabolite; also lowers seizure threshold and carries serotonin syndrome risk (particularly with SSRIs/SNRIs/MAOIs) — caution warranted, especially with polypharmacy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CYP2D6 variability</strong></td><td>Ultra-rapid metabolisers experience enhanced effect/toxicity risk; poor metabolisers experience reduced analgesic efficacy</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_fentanyl_pca_renal_copd_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":58,"question":"A patient with severe COPD and renal impairment develops severe anterior knee pain following knee surgery, inadequately controlled with simple analgesia. What is the best option for further pain management?","choices":["Fentanyl PCA","Fentanyl boluses","Ketamine infusion 0.1mg/kg/hr","Morphine","Oxycodone"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<p>This patient has two major factors limiting opioid choice: renal impairment (favouring an opioid without active, renally-cleared metabolites) and severe COPD (favouring a delivery method that allows patient-titrated, on-demand dosing to minimise the risk of oversedation and respiratory depression from fixed/nurse-administered boluses).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Correct.</strong> Fentanyl PCA combines the most renally-favourable opioid (hepatically metabolised to inactive norfentanyl, no significant accumulation in renal impairment) with a delivery method that allows the patient to self-titrate analgesia to their own pain and sedation level, minimising the respiratory depression risk that is particularly dangerous in severe COPD — patient-controlled dosing is inherently safer than fixed boluses since oversedation limits further self-dosing.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Fentanyl boluses use the same renally-favourable drug, but as nurse/clinician-administered boluses rather than patient-controlled dosing, this removes the built-in safety ceiling of PCA (the sedated patient simply doesn’t press the button) and is less responsive to the patient’s fluctuating requirements — less optimal than PCA in a high-risk respiratory patient.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Ketamine infusion 0.1mg/kg/hr is a useful adjunct in some contexts (e.g. opioid-tolerant patients, opioid-sparing strategies) but is not primarily indicated as first-line monotherapy for severe acute postoperative pain. This would be a rescue option as an add-on if fentanyl PCA fails.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Morphine accumulates significantly in renal impairment via active metabolites (M6G, M3G), risking prolonged sedation and respiratory depression — particularly hazardous in a patient with severe COPD.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Oxycodone is safer than morphine as it is mainly hepatically metabolised to noroxycodone (inactive), but it has a minor active metabolite (oxymorphone) that can accumulate in renal impairment. An oxycodone PCA would be the correct answer if fentanyl PCA were not in the choices.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Drug Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Fentanyl</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>MOA</strong></td><td>Highly selective mu-opioid receptor agonist; highly lipophilic, rapid onset, short context-sensitive half-time with single doses (prolonged with infusions due to fat redistribution)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via CYP3A4 → N-dealkylation to norfentanyl (inactive)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of inactive metabolites — no clinically significant accumulation in renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td><td>Favourable in renal impairment; rapid onset/offset suits PCA titration</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Ketamine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>MOA</strong></td><td>Non-competitive NMDA receptor antagonist (primary mechanism); also has effects at opioid receptors, monoaminergic reuptake, and voltage-gated calcium/sodium channels. Produces dissociative anaesthesia/analgesia and is a key agent for opioid-sparing and anti-hyperalgesic effect, particularly useful in opioid-tolerant states or central sensitisation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td><td>Psychomimetic side effects (hallucinations, dysphoria) limit use; caution with hepatic impairment; generally low respiratory depressant effect, which can be advantageous in COPD</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Morphine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>MOA</strong></td><td>Mu-opioid receptor agonist (main), with some kappa activity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic glucuronidation (UGT2B7, no CYP450 involvement) → morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G, active analgesic/respiratory depressant) and morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G, inactive analgesically but neuroexcitatory)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Both metabolites renally cleared</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td><td>Significant accumulation risk in renal impairment; least favourable of this group for renal/COPD patients</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Oxycodone</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>MOA</strong></td><td>Mu-opioid receptor agonist (with possible minor kappa activity); roughly 1.5x potency of oral morphine</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via CYP3A4 (major → noroxycodone, inactive) and CYP2D6 (minor → oxymorphone, active, more potent)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renally cleared parent drug and metabolites</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td><td>Accumulates in renal impairment, though often clinically better tolerated than morphine; subject to CYP2D6 genetic variability affecting oxymorphone formation</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_cannabinoid_receptors_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":59,"stem":"Regarding cannabinoid (CB) receptors and their ligands, are the following statements true or false?","choices":["Nabilone is a CB receptor antagonist.","CB2 receptors are mainly located in the central nervous system.","CB2 receptors are inducible and increase in expression during inflammation.","Anandamide can bind to TRPV1 receptors.","Presynaptic CB1 receptors can modulate GABA release."],"correct":[false,false,true,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Cannabinoid receptors (CB1 and CB2) are pre-synaptic G-protein coupled receptors that mediate the effects of endogenous cannabinoids (endocannabinoids, e.g. anandamide, 2-AG) and exogenous cannabinoid drugs, playing important roles in pain modulation, inflammation, and neurotransmission.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Nabilone is a synthetic cannabinoid and a CB1/CB2 receptor agonist (not antagonist), used clinically for chemotherapy-induced nausea/vomiting and as an adjunct in some chronic pain settings.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — CB1 receptors are the ones predominantly found in the CNS (particularly high density in areas like the hippocampus, cerebellum, and basal ganglia), mediating the classic psychoactive effects of cannabinoids. CB2 receptors are mainly found peripherally, particularly on immune cells (macrophages, microglia, lymphocytes), with comparatively limited CNS expression under normal conditions.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — CB2 receptor expression is inducible and upregulated during inflammation and tissue injury, particularly on activated microglia and immune cells, which is why CB2 is of particular interest as a target for anti-inflammatory and analgesic drug development without the psychoactive effects associated with CB1.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Anandamide is an endogenous cannabinoid that, in addition to acting at CB1/CB2 receptors, is also an agonist at TRPV1 receptors, contributing to its complex and sometimes dual pro-nociceptive/anti-nociceptive effects depending on the receptor and location involved.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Presynaptic CB1 receptors are widely expressed on both excitatory (glutamatergic) and inhibitory (GABAergic) presynaptic terminals; activation of presynaptic CB1 receptors inhibits neurotransmitter release, including GABA release, forming the basis of \"retrograde endocannabinoid signalling\" in synaptic plasticity.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Cannabinoid System</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng144'',''NG-144 Cannabis Products'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NG-144 Cannabis Products\n</button></div><p style=\"margin:14px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">The endocannabinoid system (ECS) is a homeostatic signalling network involved in nociception, mood, appetite, memory, and immune function. It comprises cannabinoid receptors, endogenous ligands (endocannabinoids), and the enzymes that synthesise and degrade them.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Receptors</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Receptor</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>CB1</strong></td><td>Densely expressed in the CNS (particularly cortex, hippocampus, cerebellum, basal ganglia), with lower peripheral expression. Mediates the psychoactive effects of THC and modulates synaptic neurotransmitter release (both excitatory and inhibitory) via presynaptic inhibition.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>CB2</strong></td><td>Less ubiquitous; acts by inhibiting proinflammatory responses of immune cells such as macrophages, lymphocytes, and microglia. Largely peripheral/immune in distribution, and upregulated in inflammatory states.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Signalling</strong></td><td>Both are G-protein coupled receptors, signalling via inhibition of adenylyl cyclase and reduced cAMP formation.</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Beyond CB1/CB2 — Emerging Targets</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>GPR55</strong></td><td>An orphan receptor and its variants have been identified as new endocannabinoid-responsive receptors, sharing numerous cannabinoid ligands with CB1 and CB2, making it a potential therapeutic target for pain.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>PPARs</strong></td><td>Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptors — nuclear transcription factors activated by cannabinoid-like molecules — have also been implicated in modulating inflammatory and neuropathic pain.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>TRPV1</strong></td><td>Transient receptor potential channels, most notably TRPV1, have a prominent role in pain and interact with the endocannabinoid system (e.g. anandamide is a TRPV1 agonist as well as a CB1/CB2 ligand).</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Ligands</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Class</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Endocannabinoids</strong></td><td>Anandamide and 2-arachidonoylglycerol (2-AG), synthesised on demand from membrane phospholipids, acting as retrograde messengers at presynaptic terminals.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Phytocannabinoids</strong></td><td>THC (partial CB1/CB2 agonist, psychoactive) and cannabidiol (CBD, non-psychoactive, complex/indirect pharmacology).</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Synthetic cannabinoids</strong></td><td>e.g. nabilone (CB1/CB2 agonist, used for chemotherapy-induced nausea/vomiting and occasionally chronic pain).</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Relevance in Pain Medicine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Preclinical</strong></td><td>Cannabinoids have shown effective anti-nociception in numerous animal models of chronic inflammatory and neuropathic pain, with evidence supporting anti-nociception from dual CB1/CB2 agonists as well as receptor-specific agonists.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical evidence</strong></td><td>Human trial evidence is more limited and heterogeneous than preclinical data, particularly for chronic non-cancer pain, with modest effect sizes and significant variability between studies.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Direction of travel</strong></td><td>Clinical interest is moving toward peripherally-restricted or receptor-selective agonists to try to separate analgesic benefit from central psychoactive/psychotomimetic side effects.</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_drug_driving_regs_2015_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":60,"stem":"These drugs are specified under the UK Drug Driving Regulations 2015, for which police may test a driver suspected of drug-impaired driving:","choices":["Methadone","Fentanyl","Tramadol","Morphine","Oxycodone"],"correct":[true,false,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>The Drug Driving (Specified Limits) (England and Wales) Regulations 2015 set defined blood concentration limits for a fixed list of drugs, split into two groups: illicit drugs with very low (\"zero tolerance\"-style) limits (e.g. cannabis, cocaine, ketamine, MDMA, LSD, heroin/6-monoacetylmorphine), and a group of commonly prescribed medicines with higher limits reflecting therapeutic use, including methadone, morphine, diazepam, oxazepam, lorazepam, temazepam, flunitrazepam, and clonazepam. Patients prescribed these drugs are not automatically committing an offence provided their driving is not impaired and, if stopped, they can show the medication was prescribed and taken as directed (a statutory \"medical defence\").</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Methadone has a specified legal limit under this legislation, reflecting its common use in opioid substitution therapy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Fentanyl is not among the drugs with a specified legal limit under the current regulations, despite being a potent opioid with driving-impairment potential — it is not part of the named list.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Tramadol is not included among the drugs with a specified legal limit under this legislation, despite being commonly prescribed and having sedative/psychomotor effects.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Morphine has a specified legal limit, relevant both to patients on prescribed opioid analgesia and to heroin users (heroin is metabolised to morphine, and 6-monoacetylmorphine — a heroin-specific marker — is separately listed with its own very low limit).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Oxycodone is not among the drugs with a specified legal limit under this legislation, despite its widespread prescription for pain.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Controlled Drugs Classification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Misuse of Drugs Act 1971: Classes A–C</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Class</th><th>Penalty Tier</th><th>Examples Relevant to Pain Medicine</th><th>Other Notable Examples</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class A</strong></td><td>Highest harm/penalty</td><td>Morphine, diamorphine (heroin), fentanyl, oxycodone, methadone, ketamine (injectable misuse context varies)</td><td>Cocaine, MDMA, LSD, magic mushrooms</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class B</strong></td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Codeine (in unlicensed/high-dose non-medicinal contexts), cannabis, pregabalin, gabapentin</td><td>Amphetamines, barbiturates, synthetic cannabinoids</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class C</strong></td><td>Lowest of the three</td><td>Tramadol, benzodiazepines (e.g. diazepam), zolpidem/zopiclone</td><td>Anabolic steroids, GHB</td></tr>\n        </table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Note: Class relates to penalties for unlawful possession/supply, not clinical prescribing rules.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001: Schedules 1–5</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Schedule</th><th>Description</th><th>Prescribing/Storage Requirements</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 1</strong></td><td>No recognised medicinal use</td><td>Possession/supply generally unlawful except under Home Office licence (research only)</td><td>LSD, raw opium (unprocessed), psilocybin</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 2</strong></td><td>Recognised medicinal use, high abuse potential</td><td>Safe custody (locked CD cabinet), CD register entry with running balance, prescription must specify form/strength/dose/total quantity in words and figures</td><td>Morphine, diamorphine, fentanyl, oxycodone, methadone, ketamine, amphetamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 3</strong></td><td>Recognised medicinal use, lower abuse potential than Sch 2</td><td>CD prescription-writing rules apply (words + figures); most exempt from safe custody (tramadol, midazolam, gabapentin, pregabalin, phenobarbital exempt); no CD register requirement</td><td>Tramadol, midazolam, gabapentin, pregabalin, temazepam, buprenorphine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 4</strong></td><td>Part 1: benzodiazepines; Part 2: anabolic steroids</td><td>No CD prescription-writing requirements; no safe custody; no register</td><td>Diazepam, lorazepam, testosterone</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 5</strong></td><td>Very low abuse potential preparations</td><td>Minimal restrictions; can be sold OTC in some cases</td><td>Codeine</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_controlled_drugs_classification_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":61,"stem":"Regarding the classification of these analgesics under UK controlled drug legislation (Misuse of Drugs Act 1971 and Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001):","choices":["Methadone is a Schedule 2 controlled drug requiring safe custody storage.","Fentanyl is classified as a Class B substance under the Misuse of Drugs Act 1971.","Tramadol is a Schedule 3 controlled drug.","Morphine prescriptions must state the total quantity to be supplied in both words and figures.","Oxycodone is a Schedule 4 controlled drug, in the same schedule as benzodiazepines."],"correct":[true,false,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p>UK controlled drugs are classified in two overlapping systems: the Misuse of Drugs Act 1971 assigns a Class (A, B, C) reflecting perceived harm and penalty tier, while the Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001 assigns a Schedule (1–5) governing prescribing, storage, and record-keeping requirements.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Methadone is a Schedule 2 controlled drug. Schedule 2 CDs (including morphine, oxycodone, fentanyl, methadone) require secure (safe custody) storage in a CD cabinet and must be recorded in a CD register with a running balance.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Fentanyl is a Class A substance, reflecting its high potency and abuse/harm potential — the same class as morphine, oxycodone, methadone, and diamorphine.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — Tramadol is a Schedule 3 controlled drug. Unlike most Schedule 3 drugs, tramadol is exempt from the safe custody requirement, though standard CD prescription-writing requirements still apply, and it does not require entry in the CD register.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — For Schedule 2 (and Schedule 3) controlled drugs, UK prescription requirements mandate that the prescriber specify the drug, form, strength, dose, and total quantity in both words and figures, along with being handwritten (or otherwise validated) and dated, to reduce the risk of alteration or diversion.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Oxycodone is a Schedule 2 controlled drug, not Schedule 4. Schedule 4 contains drugs such as benzodiazepines (Part 1) and anabolic steroids (Part 2), which have a lower CD prescribing/storage burden than Schedule 2 drugs like oxycodone.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Controlled Drugs Classification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Misuse of Drugs Act 1971: Classes A–C</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Class</th><th>Penalty Tier</th><th>Examples Relevant to Pain Medicine</th><th>Other Notable Examples</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class A</strong></td><td>Highest harm/penalty</td><td>Morphine, diamorphine (heroin), fentanyl, oxycodone, methadone, ketamine (injectable misuse context varies)</td><td>Cocaine, MDMA, LSD, magic mushrooms</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class B</strong></td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Codeine (in unlicensed/high-dose non-medicinal contexts), cannabis, pregabalin, gabapentin</td><td>Amphetamines, barbiturates, synthetic cannabinoids</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class C</strong></td><td>Lowest of the three</td><td>Tramadol, benzodiazepines (e.g. diazepam), zolpidem/zopiclone</td><td>Anabolic steroids, GHB</td></tr>\n        </table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Note: Class relates to penalties for unlawful possession/supply, not clinical prescribing rules.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001: Schedules 1–5</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Schedule</th><th>Description</th><th>Prescribing/Storage Requirements</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 1</strong></td><td>No recognised medicinal use</td><td>Possession/supply generally unlawful except under Home Office licence (research only)</td><td>LSD, raw opium (unprocessed), psilocybin</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 2</strong></td><td>Recognised medicinal use, high abuse potential</td><td>Safe custody (locked CD cabinet), CD register entry with running balance, prescription must specify form/strength/dose/total quantity in words and figures</td><td>Morphine, diamorphine, fentanyl, oxycodone, methadone, ketamine, amphetamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 3</strong></td><td>Recognised medicinal use, lower abuse potential than Sch 2</td><td>CD prescription-writing rules apply (words + figures); most exempt from safe custody (tramadol, midazolam, gabapentin, pregabalin, phenobarbital exempt); no CD register requirement</td><td>Tramadol, midazolam, gabapentin, pregabalin, temazepam, buprenorphine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 4</strong></td><td>Part 1: benzodiazepines; Part 2: anabolic steroids</td><td>No CD prescription-writing requirements; no safe custody; no register</td><td>Diazepam, lorazepam, testosterone</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 5</strong></td><td>Very low abuse potential preparations</td><td>Minimal restrictions; can be sold OTC in some cases</td><td>Codeine</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_opioid_qtc_prolongation_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":62,"stem":"Which of these prolong QTc?","choices":["Hydromorphone","Tapentadol","Pethidine","Methadone","Alfentanil"],"correct":[false,false,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>QTc prolongation is a clinically important consideration in opioid prescribing, as it increases the risk of torsades de pointes and sudden cardiac death; the risk varies markedly between opioids depending on their effect on cardiac potassium channels (hERG channel blockade), and this variability is a well-recognised exam theme.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Hydromorphone is not significantly associated with clinically relevant QTc prolongation and is considered one of the safer opioid choices where QTc prolongation is a concern.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Tapentadol has not been shown to cause clinically significant QTc prolongation at therapeutic doses, unlike some other opioids.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Pethidine is not classically associated with QTc prolongation as its primary cardiac concern; its more notable risks relate to its metabolite norpethidine (neuroexcitatory, seizure risk in accumulation) and serotonergic interactions (serotonin syndrome risk with MAOIs/SSRIs), rather than QTc effects.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Methadone is the opioid most strongly and consistently associated with dose-dependent QTc prolongation, via direct blockade of the cardiac hERG potassium channel. This is a well-established risk requiring baseline and periodic ECG monitoring, particularly at higher doses, with other risk factors (electrolyte disturbance, other QT-prolonging drugs), or with rapid dose escalation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Alfentanil is not significantly associated with QTc prolongation and is generally considered cardiac-safe in this respect, consistent with other fentanyl-derivative opioids.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Opioid Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Hydromorphone</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Semi-synthetic mu-opioid receptor agonist; ~5–7x potency of oral morphine</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic glucuronidation (UGT) → hydromorphone-3-glucuronide (H3G, inactive analgesically, neuroexcitatory) — no significant CYP450 involvement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td>No significant QTc effect</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>Useful alternative to morphine in mild-moderate renal impairment (though H3G still accumulates, requiring caution); available in high-concentration formulations useful in palliative care for small-volume dosing</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Tapentadol</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Dual-mechanism analgesic — mu-opioid receptor agonist and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (NRI); minimal serotonergic activity, distinguishing it from tramadol</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily glucuronidation (UGT) — minimal CYP450 involvement, few active metabolites</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td>No significant QTc effect</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>Lower incidence of nausea/vomiting and constipation than classical opioids at equianalgesic doses, attributed to lower mu-receptor occupancy for equivalent analgesia; lower serotonin syndrome risk than tramadol due to minimal serotonergic activity; used in both acute nociceptive and neuropathic pain</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Pethidine (Meperidine)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Synthetic mu-opioid receptor agonist with additional local anaesthetic-like (sodium channel blocking) and anticholinergic/antimuscarinic properties</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via N-demethylation → norpethidine, an active, neuroexcitatory metabolite (lowers seizure threshold, causes tremor/myoclonus/agitation) that accumulates with repeated dosing or renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal (norpethidine notably renally cleared and accumulates in renal impairment)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td>Not classically associated with QTc prolongation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>Significant serotonergic activity — contraindicated with MAOIs (risk of severe serotonin syndrome/hyperpyrexic reactions); historically used in labour analgesia, now largely superseded due to norpethidine toxicity risk and neonatal effects</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Methadone</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Synthetic mu-opioid receptor agonist and NMDA receptor antagonist; also inhibits monoamine (serotonin/noradrenaline) reuptake</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (also CYP2B6, CYP2D6) → inactive metabolites; highly variable interpatient pharmacokinetics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Faecal/biliary predominant (advantageous in renal impairment) with a minor renal component</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Long and highly variable half-life (8–60+ hours), poor correlation between plasma level and effect, risk of delayed accumulation/toxicity with dose titration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td><strong>Most significant opioid cause of dose-dependent QTc prolongation</strong> via hERG potassium channel blockade — requires baseline/periodic ECG monitoring, particularly at higher doses or with other risk factors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>NMDA antagonism provides additional utility in neuropathic pain and opioid tolerance/hyperalgesia; complex drug interactions via CYP3A4 (many drugs alter methadone levels)</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Alfentanil</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Highly selective mu-opioid receptor agonist; synthetic fentanyl analogue</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via CYP3A4 → inactive metabolites</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal (inactive metabolites — no clinically significant accumulation in renal impairment)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Rapid onset, short duration of action due to small volume of distribution and rapid redistribution/clearance — shortest context-sensitive half-time of the fentanyl-family opioids, making it useful for short procedures</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td>No significant QTc effect</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>Primarily used intraoperatively/for short procedures rather than chronic pain management; potency roughly 1/4–1/10 that of fentanyl but faster onset/offset</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_stellate_ganglion_consent_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":63,"stem":"Which complications should be discussed at consent for a stellate ganglion block?","choices":["Pneumothorax","Constipation","Gastroparesis","Hypertension","Hoarseness of voice"],"correct":[true,false,false,false,true],"explanation":"<p>The stellate ganglion (cervicothoracic sympathetic ganglion) lies close to several important structures at the base of the neck, and consent should cover both common minor side effects (expected sympathetic blockade features) and rarer but serious procedural risks related to needle trajectory and local spread of injectate.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Pneumothorax is a recognised risk due to the close proximity of the lung apex to the injection target, particularly with a lower (anterior C7) approach; careful needle depth control and image guidance reduce but do not eliminate this risk.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Constipation is not a recognised complication of stellate ganglion block; it has no established mechanism of action on gut motility relevant to this procedure.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Gastroparesis is not a recognised complication of stellate ganglion block; this is not an expected effect of cervical sympathetic blockade.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — Sympathetic blockade from a stellate ganglion block causes vasodilation in the blocked territory, and hypotension (not hypertension) is the more relevant/recognised haemodynamic consideration, alongside the expected localised effects of Horner’s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) from sympathetic interruption to the head/neck/upper limb.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Hoarseness of voice is a recognised risk due to local spread of anaesthetic to the recurrent laryngeal nerve, which runs close to the injection site; this is usually transient but should be specifically consented for, along with the associated risk of subjective dysphagia/risk of aspiration while the effect lasts.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Stellate Ganglion</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Anatomy Background</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">The stellate ganglion is formed by fusion of the inferior cervical and first thoracic sympathetic ganglia (present in ~80% of people; when unfused, only the inferior cervical ganglion is blocked). It lies anterior to the neck of the first rib and the transverse process of C7, close to the vertebral artery, longus colli muscle, carotid sheath, recurrent laryngeal nerve, and lung apex — most anaesthetists target the C6 (Chassaignac''s tubercle) level anteriorly to reduce pneumothorax/vertebral artery injection risk while still achieving adequate spread to block the ganglion at C7/T1.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Indications</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Sympathetically-maintained pain</strong></td><td>Complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS) of the upper limb; post-herpetic neuralgia (head, neck, upper limb distribution); phantom limb pain (upper limb); refractory angina (adjunctive)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Vascular</strong></td><td>Raynaud''s phenomenon/peripheral vascular insufficiency of the upper limb; vasospastic disorders, upper limb ischaemia; post-traumatic vasospasm, frostbite</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Other</strong></td><td>Hyperhidrosis (upper limb/facial); refractory ventricular arrhythmias/electrical storm (cardiac sympathetic denervation); some vasomotor symptom indications (e.g. menopausal hot flushes) — emerging/off-label; PTSD (emerging, off-label, investigational)</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Contraindications</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Absolute</strong></td><td>Patient refusal/inability to consent; local infection at injection site; coagulopathy/significant bleeding diathesis (relative-absolute depending on severity, given proximity to major vessels); recent MI or uncontrolled cardiac arrhythmia; contralateral phrenic nerve palsy or contralateral pneumothorax; contralateral recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Relative</strong></td><td>Severe respiratory disease/compromised pulmonary reserve; glaucoma (Horner''s syndrome effects); allergy to local anaesthetic; anticoagulation (per standard regional anaesthesia guidance on interruption)</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Complications</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Category</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Expected/anticipated</strong></td><td>Horner''s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos) — confirms successful block; nasal congestion (Guttman''s sign); conjunctival injection; temperature/colour change and increased skin temperature in the blocked limb</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Recognised complications</strong></td><td>Hoarseness of voice (recurrent laryngeal nerve, usually transient); dysphagia; pneumothorax (lung apex proximity); haematoma (vascular puncture); intravascular injection (especially vertebral artery — seizure/LOC even with tiny volumes); intrathecal/epidural spread (high or total spinal); brachial plexus block (spread too lateral/posterior); phrenic nerve block (hemidiaphragmatic paresis); infection; bradycardia (cardiac accelerator fibre blockade)</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_stellate_ganglion_c6_us_anatomy_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":64,"stem":"Regarding stellate ganglion block at C6 using ultrasound:","choices":["The target injection point lies anterior to longus colli muscle.","The carotid sheath lies posterior to the target injection point.","The oesophagus lies medial to the target injection point.","The thoracic duct lies medial to the target injection point.","The dome of the lung lies inferior to the target injection point."],"correct":[true,false,true,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Ultrasound-guided stellate ganglion block is typically performed at the C6 level (Chassaignac''s tubercle), where injectate is deposited into the fascial plane between the prevertebral fascia and longus colli muscle, allowing spread down to the stellate ganglion at C7/T1 while avoiding a deeper, riskier approach directly at C7.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — The injection target is the fascial plane anterior to longus colli muscle, deep to the carotid sheath and sternocleidomastoid, allowing safe deposition of local anaesthetic away from the vertebral artery (which runs within the transverse foramen, posterior to longus colli).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — The carotid sheath (containing the carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and vagus nerve) lies lateral to the target injection point, not posterior. On ultrasound, the sheath and its contents are identified and retracted/avoided laterally before advancing the needle to the prevertebral fascia/longus colli plane.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — The oesophagus lies medial to the target, tucked between the trachea and prevertebral fascia; it is of particular relevance on the left side, where the oesophagus can deviate further leftward, increasing the risk of inadvertent puncture or injection with a left-sided block.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — The thoracic duct lies medially and inferiorly, entering the venous system near the junction of the left subclavian and internal jugular veins; it is a specifically recognised risk structure for left-sided stellate ganglion blocks, with chylothorax a rare reported complication.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — The dome (apex) of the lung lies inferior to the C6-level injection site, which is part of the rationale for performing the block at C6 rather than lower at C7 — reducing (though not eliminating) the risk of pneumothorax.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Stellate Ganglion</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\">\n        <div style=\"position:relative;width:100%;padding-top:56.25%;border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;background:#000\">\n          <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/B6ROeVFUJSY\" title=\"Stellate Ganglion Block\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;border:0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen></iframe>\n        </div>\n      </div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_ketamine_general_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":65,"stem":"Ketamine:","choices":["Ketamine is available clinically as a racemic mixture of S and R isomers.","Chronic ketamine use is associated with toxicity to the urinary tract.","The R(-) isomer of ketamine is more potent than the S(+) isomer.","The S(+) isomer is associated with greater psychomimetic effects than the R(-) isomer.","Ketamine has local anaesthetic-like effects via blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels."],"correct":[true,true,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine is a phencyclidine derivative, primarily known for NMDA receptor antagonism producing dissociative anaesthesia and analgesia, though it has several other receptor and channel interactions contributing to its overall clinical effect.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Standard clinical ketamine preparations are a racemic mixture of the S(+) and R(-) enantiomers; a single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) preparation is also available separately, roughly twice as potent as the racemate.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Chronic/heavy ketamine use is well recognised to cause urinary tract toxicity, specifically \"ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis,\" causing severe lower urinary tract symptoms (frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria), reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis and renal impairment secondary to bladder pathology.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — It is the S(+) isomer, not R(-), that is more potent — the S(+) isomer has higher affinity for the NMDA receptor and is approximately 2–4 times more potent as an analgesic/anaesthetic than the R(-) isomer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — The S(+) isomer has greater psychomimetic activity than the R(-) isomer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — In addition to its principal NMDA receptor antagonism, ketamine also has local anaesthetic-like properties via blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels, contributing to its analgesic effect and providing a pharmacological basis for its occasional use (typically at higher/local concentrations) in modulating neuronal excitability beyond the NMDA pathway alone; ketamine also interacts with opioid receptors, monoaminergic reuptake, and voltage-gated calcium channels, reflecting its broad multimodal pharmacology.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_ketamine_isomer_potency_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":66,"stem":"Regarding ketamine, are the following statements true or false?","choices":["It acts on the central nervous system.","It has local anaesthetic properties.","S(+) ketamine is four times as potent as the racemate.","S(+) ketamine and R(-) ketamine have the same affinity for NMDA receptors.","Ketamine has two geometric isomers."],"correct":[true,true,false,false,false],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine is a phencyclidine derivative acting primarily as a non-competitive NMDA receptor antagonist, producing dissociative anaesthesia, analgesia, and a range of other CNS and peripheral effects, alongside a distinct pharmacokinetic and isomer-specific profile.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine acts centrally, principally via NMDA receptor antagonism in the CNS, producing its characteristic dissociative anaesthetic, analgesic, amnesic, and psychomimetic effects; it also has effects at opioid, monoaminergic, and muscarinic receptors within the CNS.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine has local anaesthetic-like properties via blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels, contributing an additional analgesic mechanism beyond its central NMDA antagonism.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — S(+) ketamine is approximately twice as potent as the racemate (not four times), reflecting that the racemate is a 50:50 mix of the more potent S(+) and less potent R(-) isomers — S(+) alone therefore has roughly double the potency of the mixed preparation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — S(+) and R(-) ketamine do not have the same NMDA receptor affinity — S(+) ketamine has a higher affinity for the NMDA receptor (and is more potent) than R(-) ketamine, which underlies the difference in their clinical potency and side-effect profiles.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine''s S(+) and R(-) forms are enantiomers (optical/stereoisomers), arising from a single chiral centre, not geometric (cis-trans) isomers — geometric isomerism requires restricted rotation (e.g. around a double bond or ring), which is not the basis of ketamine''s isomerism.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_ketamine_receptor_binding_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":67,"stem":"Ketamine binds to:","choices":["NMDA receptors.","Muscarinic receptors.","Nicotinic receptors.","Opioid receptors.","Neuronal potassium channels, to exhibit its local anaesthetic properties."],"correct":[true,true,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine has a broad receptor and channel interaction profile beyond its principal NMDA antagonism, which underlies much of its diverse clinical effect and side-effect spectrum.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine''s principal mechanism is non-competitive antagonism at the NMDA receptor, binding within the open ion channel pore, underlying its dissociative anaesthetic and analgesic effects.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine has affinity for muscarinic acetylcholine receptors (antagonist activity), contributing to some of its anticholinergic-type effects and possibly its emergence phenomena/delirium profile.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine also inhibits nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, adding to its broad receptor interaction profile beyond the NMDA system.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine has weak affinity at opioid receptors (mu, kappa, and sigma sites have all been implicated), contributing modestly to its overall analgesic effect alongside NMDA antagonism.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine''s local anaesthetic-like properties arise from blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels, not potassium channels — this sodium channel effect underlies its use-dependent membrane-stabilising action, contributing an additional analgesic mechanism distinct from NMDA antagonism.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_ketamine_neurological_effects_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":68,"stem":"Ketamine:","choices":["S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolism and maintains cerebrovascular autoregulation.","Ketamine triggers seizure activity.","Ketamine is contraindicated in patients with epilepsy.","Ketamine may have neuroprotective and neuroregenerative effects.","Neuroprotection appears to be due to increased calcium fluxes and glutamate accumulation at the NMDA receptor."],"correct":[true,false,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine''s neurological effects have been historically over-simplified and misunderstood — older teaching emphasised raised intracranial pressure and epileptogenic risk, but more recent evidence has substantially revised this view, particularly for the S(+) isomer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — S(+) ketamine, unlike the racemate at higher doses, has been shown to reduce cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity, challenging the older dogma that all ketamine formulations uniformly raise cerebral metabolism and intracranial pressure.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine does not reliably trigger clinical seizure activity. While it can produce excitatory EEG changes (particularly in limbic structures) at a neurophysiological level, this does not translate into a clinically significant proconvulsant effect, and ketamine is in fact used as a treatment option in refractory status epilepticus.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine is not contraindicated in patients with epilepsy. Given its use in treating refractory status epilepticus and lack of reliable proconvulsant clinical effect, it can be used cautiously in this population, in contrast to older teaching that avoided ketamine in epilepsy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine may have neuroprotective and neuroregenerative effects, an area of active research interest — proposed mechanisms include reduction of excitotoxic injury via NMDA antagonism, anti-inflammatory effects, and (in psychiatric/depression research) promotion of synaptogenesis via downstream BDNF/mTOR pathway activation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — The proposed mechanism of neuroprotection is the opposite of what''s stated — ketamine''s NMDA receptor antagonism reduces excessive calcium influx and glutamate-mediated excitotoxicity (the process that causes neuronal injury in ischaemia/trauma), rather than increasing it; this reduction in excitotoxic calcium/glutamate activity is the proposed protective mechanism.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_ketamine_systemic_effects_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":69,"stem":"Ketamine:","choices":["Ketamine has a central sympathetic effect.","Ketamine has no direct myocardial depression.","S(+) ketamine is a better choice than R(-) ketamine in treating acute asthma.","Ketamine is absolutely contraindicated in patients with chronic renal failure.","Ketamine may have immunomodulating properties."],"correct":[true,false,false,false,true],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine''s cardiovascular, respiratory, renal, and immune effects are frequently misunderstood, as its clinically observed profile (typically stimulatory) can mask underlying direct depressant actions.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine causes central sympathetic stimulation, via inhibition of catecholamine (noradrenaline) reuptake centrally and peripherally, which is the mechanism behind its characteristic increases in heart rate, blood pressure, and cardiac output.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine does have a direct myocardial depressant effect, but in most patients this is normally masked by its more dominant central sympathetic stimulation, resulting in a net stimulatory cardiovascular effect. In catecholamine-depleted states (e.g. critical illness, sepsis, chronic pain/adrenal exhaustion) where sympathetic reserve is reduced, this underlying direct depressant effect can become unmasked, causing unexpected hypotension and reduced contractility.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — There is no clear evidence that S(+) ketamine is superior to R(-) ketamine specifically for bronchodilation in acute asthma; ketamine''s bronchodilator effect (useful in refractory status asthmaticus) is attributed to both sympathomimetic activity and direct relaxant effects on bronchial smooth muscle, but isomer-specific superiority in this indication is not established, making this statement false.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine is not absolutely contraindicated in chronic renal failure; its metabolites are not major clinical concerns in the way that, for example, morphine or pethidine metabolites are, and it remains a usable analgesic/anaesthetic option in renal impairment with appropriate caution and dose consideration.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine has been shown to have immunomodulating properties, including anti-inflammatory effects (attenuation of pro-inflammatory cytokine release such as TNF-alpha and IL-6), which is of interest in contexts such as sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response modulation, though the full clinical significance remains an area of ongoing research.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_noradrenaline_reuptake_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":71,"stem":"Noradrenaline neurotransmitter reuptake happens through:","choices":["Oxidative degradation","Enzymatic degradation","Hydrolysis","Reuptake by the presynaptic neuron","Diffusion into the vascular circulation"],"correct":[true,true,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Termination of noradrenaline''s action at the sympathetic synapse occurs through several complementary mechanisms, rather than a single dominant process.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Noradrenaline undergoes oxidative degradation via <strong>monoamine oxidase (MAO)</strong>, located on the outer mitochondrial membrane within the presynaptic neuron, converting noradrenaline into inactive metabolites.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Noradrenaline also undergoes enzymatic degradation via <strong>catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)</strong>, located extraneuronally (postsynaptically and in other tissues such as the liver), methylating noradrenaline into inactive metabolites (normetanephrine and ultimately VMA).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Hydrolysis is not a relevant mechanism for noradrenaline termination — this describes the mechanism by which <strong>acetylcholine</strong> is broken down by acetylcholinesterase at cholinergic synapses, not the mechanism for catecholamine neurotransmitters like noradrenaline.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Reuptake by the presynaptic neuron (<strong>Uptake-1</strong>, via the noradrenaline transporter, NET) is the <strong>most significant and rapid mechanism</strong> terminating noradrenaline''s synaptic action, recycling the majority of released noradrenaline back into presynaptic storage vesicles for re-use.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — A proportion of released noradrenaline <strong>diffuses away from the synaptic cleft into the surrounding vasculature/extracellular fluid</strong> (sometimes termed \"Uptake-2\" when taken up by non-neuronal tissues, or simple diffusional loss into the circulation), where it is diluted and eventually metabolised systemically (e.g. hepatic MAO/COMT), contributing modestly to termination of its local synaptic effect.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Autonomic Nervous System</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Combined Reference Table</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Sympathetic</th><th>Parasympathetic</th></tr><tr><td>Spinal outflow</td><td>Thoracolumbar (T1&ndash;L2)</td><td>Craniosacral (CN III, VII, IX, X; S2&ndash;S4)</td></tr><tr><td>Preganglionic neuron length</td><td>Short</td><td>Long</td></tr><tr><td>Preganglionic neurotransmitter</td><td>Acetylcholine</td><td>Acetylcholine</td></tr><tr><td>Preganglionic receptor (on ganglion)</td><td>Nicotinic (N<sub>n</sub>)</td><td>Nicotinic (N<sub>n</sub>)</td></tr><tr><td>Ganglion location</td><td>Close to spinal cord (paravertebral chain / prevertebral ganglia, e.g. coeliac, mesenteric)</td><td>Close to, or within, the target organ wall (e.g. cardiac plexus, myenteric plexus, ciliary ganglion)</td></tr><tr><td>Postganglionic neuron length</td><td>Long</td><td>Short</td></tr><tr><td>Postganglionic neurotransmitter (most targets)</td><td>Noradrenaline</td><td>Acetylcholine</td></tr><tr><td>Postganglionic receptor (most targets)</td><td>Alpha (&alpha;1, &alpha;2) / Beta (&beta;1, &beta;2, &beta;3) adrenoreceptors</td><td>Muscarinic (M1&ndash;M5)</td></tr><tr><td>Exception &mdash; sweat glands (eccrine)</td><td>Sympathetic, but the postganglionic transmitter is <strong>acetylcholine</strong> &rarr; muscarinic receptors</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr><tr><td>Exception &mdash; adrenal medulla</td><td>Preganglionic fibre synapses <strong>directly</strong> on chromaffin cells (no postganglionic neuron); releases ACh &rarr; nicotinic receptors; chromaffin cells then secrete adrenaline (~80%) and noradrenaline (~20%) into the bloodstream as hormones</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Neurotransmitters &amp; receptors of the pre- and postganglionic neurons</div><div style=\"text-align:center\"><img src=\"images/autonomic-preganglionic-postganglionic-neurons.png\" alt=\"Diagram comparing the parasympathetic and sympathetic nervous systems — preganglionic and postganglionic fibre lengths with acetylcholine, noradrenaline, nicotinic, muscarinic and adrenergic receptors marked\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:0;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_nociceptor_sensitisation_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":72,"stem":"Nociceptors are sensitised by:","choices":["Acetylcholine","Potassium","ATP","Prostaglandins","Substance P"],"correct":[false,true,true,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Peripheral sensitisation occurs when tissue damage/inflammation releases a mixture of chemical mediators &mdash; often termed the \"inflammatory soup\" &mdash; that act directly on nociceptor terminals to lower their activation threshold and/or increase their responsiveness, contributing to primary hyperalgesia.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Acetylcholine is not a classical component of the inflammatory soup implicated in nociceptor sensitisation; its physiological role relates to autonomic/neuromuscular transmission rather than peripheral pain sensitisation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Potassium (K<sup>+</sup>) is released from damaged cells and directly activates as well as sensitises nociceptor terminals, lowering the threshold for action potential generation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — ATP, released from damaged cells, acts on <strong>P2X</strong> (ligand-gated ion channel) and <strong>P2Y</strong> (GPCR) purinergic receptors on nociceptor terminals, contributing directly to sensitisation and pain signalling.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Prostaglandins (particularly PGE2), synthesised via the cyclooxygenase pathway from arachidonic acid, sensitise nociceptors by lowering their activation threshold (largely via a second-messenger-mediated reduction in the threshold of voltage-gated sodium channels) &mdash; the basis for NSAID/COX-inhibitor analgesic action.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Substance P, released from nociceptor terminals themselves (part of \"neurogenic inflammation\"), acts on <strong>NK1 receptors</strong>, contributing to vasodilation, plasma extravasation, and mast cell degranulation, amplifying local sensitisation in a feed-forward manner.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Inflammatory soup</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center\">Endogenous inflammatory mediators</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Substance</strong></td><td><strong>Source</strong></td><td><strong>Effect on nociceptor</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Potassium</td><td>Damaged cells</td><td>Activation</td></tr><tr><td>Protons</td><td>Hypoxic cells</td><td>Activation</td></tr><tr><td>Serotonin (5-HT)</td><td>Platelets</td><td>Activation</td></tr><tr><td>Bradykinin</td><td>Plasma kininogen</td><td>Activation</td></tr><tr><td>Histamine</td><td>Mast cells</td><td>Activation</td></tr><tr><td>Prostanoids</td><td>Damaged cells</td><td>Sensitization</td></tr><tr><td>Leukotrienes</td><td>Damaged cells</td><td>Sensitization</td></tr><tr><td>Substance P</td><td>Primary afferents</td><td>Sensitization</td></tr><tr><td>CGRP</td><td>Primary afferents</td><td>Sensitization</td></tr><tr><td>Glutamate</td><td>Primary afferents</td><td>Sensitization</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_inhibitory_neurotransmitters_pain_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":73,"stem":"The following neurotransmitters are inhibitory within the pain pathway in the CNS:","choices":["Acetylcholine","Glycine","GABA","Glutamate","Noradrenaline"],"correct":[true,true,true,false,true],"explanation":"<p>Inhibitory control of pain transmission in the CNS occurs both segmentally (within the spinal cord dorsal horn) and via descending pathways from supraspinal centres, involving several distinct neurotransmitter systems.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Although generally an excitatory neurotransmitter, acetylcholine is inhibitory within the pain pathway, acting mainly via muscarinic receptors (particularly M2/M4 subtypes) in the dorsal horn and supraspinally (e.g. periaqueductal grey). It has an antinociceptive/inhibitory effect on pain transmission &mdash; the pharmacological basis for the analgesic effect of intrathecal/epidural neostigmine (an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that raises synaptic ACh levels).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Glycine is a major segmental inhibitory neurotransmitter in the dorsal horn, acting on glycine receptors (ligand-gated chloride channels) to hyperpolarise second-order neurons and inhibit nociceptive transmission; glycinergic disinhibition is implicated in mechanical allodynia.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — GABA is a principal segmental inhibitory neurotransmitter in the dorsal horn, acting on GABA-A (ionotropic, fast) and GABA-B (metabotropic, slower) receptors to inhibit nociceptive transmission; loss of GABAergic tone is implicated in central sensitisation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — Glutamate is the principal <strong>excitatory</strong> neurotransmitter of the pain pathway, acting on NMDA, AMPA, and kainate receptors on dorsal horn neurons to mediate fast excitatory nociceptive transmission &mdash; the opposite of inhibitory.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Noradrenaline, released via descending pathways from the locus coeruleus, acts on alpha-2 adrenoreceptors on dorsal horn neurons and primary afferent terminals to inhibit nociceptive transmission &mdash; the pharmacological basis for the analgesic effects of clonidine and dexmedetomidine. Noradrenaline is generally an excitatory neurotransmitter but is inhibitory within the context of the pain pathway.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Inhibitory Neurotransmitters</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center\">Inhibitory neurotransmitters in the pain pathway</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Neurotransmitter</strong></td><td><strong>Full name(s)</strong></td><td><strong>Receptor in dorsal horn</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>GABA</strong></td><td>Gamma-aminobutyric acid</td><td>GABA<sub>A</sub>, GABA<sub>B</sub></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gly</strong></td><td>Glycine</td><td>Glycine (strychnine-sensitive)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Endorphins</strong></td><td>&beta;-endorphins</td><td>Opioid (&mu;&gt;&delta;,&kappa;)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Enkephalins</strong></td><td>Met- and Leu-enkephalin, Enk-8</td><td>Opioid (&delta;&gt;&mu;,&kappa;)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dynorphins</strong></td><td>Dynorphin-A, Dynorphin-B</td><td>Opioid (&kappa;&gt;&mu;,&delta;)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Endocannabinoids</strong></td><td>Anandamide (AEA), 2-arachidonoylglycerol (2-AG)</td><td>CB<sub>1</sub></td></tr><tr><td><strong>5-HT</strong></td><td>5-hydroxytryptamine (serotonin)</td><td>5-HT receptor subtypes 1&ndash;3</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NA</strong></td><td>Noradrenaline</td><td>&alpha;<sub>2</sub>-adrenergic</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_dorsal_horn_cytoarchitecture_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":74,"stem":"Regarding dorsal horn cytoarchitecture:","choices":["Galanin is excitatory in the dorsal horn","Most nociceptor afferents terminate in laminae III–V","Wide dynamic range (WDR) neurons only receive input from nociceptors","Lamina II (substantia gelatinosa) predominantly receives unmyelinated C-fibre input","Lamina I contains nociceptive-specific neurons"],"correct":[false,false,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p>The dorsal horn is organised into distinct laminae (Rexed laminae I&ndash;VI), each with characteristic afferent input and neuronal types, and this cytoarchitecture underpins how different classes of sensory information are processed and modulated.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Galanin is predominantly <strong>inhibitory/antinociceptive</strong> in the dorsal horn under normal physiological conditions, acting via <strong>GalR1</strong> receptors. Following peripheral nerve injury, galanin expression is markedly upregulated in primary afferents (a classic neuropathic pain marker), and its net effect becomes more complex &mdash; with some pronociceptive contribution via <strong>GalR2</strong> in the injured state &mdash; but as a general statement, galanin is not classified as excitatory.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Most nociceptive primary afferents (A&delta; and C fibres) terminate in the <strong>superficial dorsal horn &mdash; laminae I and II</strong>, not laminae III&ndash;V. Laminae III&ndash;V receive predominantly non-nociceptive (A&beta;, low-threshold mechanoreceptor) input, though lamina V does contain wide dynamic range neurons that also receive convergent nociceptive input.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Wide dynamic range (WDR) neurons, found mainly in lamina V, are defined precisely by receiving <strong>convergent input from both nociceptive (A&delta;, C fibre) and non-nociceptive (A&beta;, low-threshold) afferents</strong> &mdash; this convergence is the basis of their name and their proposed role in phenomena like referred pain and temporal summation (\"wind-up\").</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Lamina II (<strong>substantia gelatinosa</strong>) predominantly receives unmyelinated <strong>C-fibre</strong> input (along with some A&delta; input), and contains densely packed interneurons important in local modulation of nociceptive transmission, including the \"gate control\" mechanism.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Lamina I (marginal zone) contains <strong>nociceptive-specific neurons</strong>, which respond selectively to noxious stimuli and receive input mainly from A&delta; and C fibres, projecting via the spinothalamic tract to supraspinal pain-processing centres.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Dorsal horn</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Pain primary afferents enter the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, synapsing ipsilaterally with 2nd order neurons.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">In cross section, the spinal cord resembles an oval of white matter (myelinated axons), surrounding an H-shaped centre of grey matter (cell bodies).</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">The grey matter is arranged into ten histological layers, or laminae, based on cytological architecture, known as Rexed''s laminae.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">The dorsal horn is made up of laminae I&ndash;V.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"text-align:center\"><img src=\"images/rexed-laminae.png\" alt=\"Rexed laminae — spinal cord grey matter cross-section with laminae I–X labelled alongside a table of regions, nuclei and corresponding laminae\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:0;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">A&delta; primary afferents terminate on laminae I and V, and C fibres terminate on lamina II, the substantia gelatinosa. A&delta; primary afferents synapse directly with secondary afferents that will eventually carry the pain signal to the thalamus. C fibres do not synapse directly with secondary afferents, but connect instead with interneurons that carry the signal on to secondary afferents in laminae I or V. These interneurons are important in modulation of the pain signal.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Visceral input differs in that fewer primary afferents activate a larger number of second order neurons, resulting in poorer localisation of pain. Visceral afferents also converge with somatic inputs, which may account for the phenomenon of referred pain.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_central_sensitisation_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":75,"stem":"Regarding central sensitisation:","choices":["Wind-up is a heterosynaptic phenomenon","NMDA receptor activation is integral to the development of central sensitisation","Central sensitisation involves only peripheral changes","Central sensitisation can result in pain from normally non-painful stimuli","Central sensitisation is dependent on repeated or sustained afferent input (temporal summation)"],"correct":[false,true,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Central sensitisation is an activity-dependent increase in the excitability and synaptic efficacy of neurons in the central nociceptive pathways, following intense, repeated, or sustained peripheral input, leading to amplified pain responses that outlast and outsize the initiating stimulus.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Wind-up is a <strong>homosynaptic</strong> phenomenon &mdash; it refers to the progressive, frequency-dependent increase in dorsal horn neuron output in response to repeated stimulation of the <strong>same</strong> C-fibre input at a constant intensity, occurring at the same synapse. Heterosynaptic changes (affecting adjacent, non-stimulated synapses/inputs) are a feature of broader central sensitisation but not of wind-up itself, which is a more specific, shorter-lived electrophysiological phenomenon considered an early contributor to central sensitisation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — NMDA receptor activation is central (and essentially integral) to the development of central sensitisation &mdash; sustained/repetitive glutamate release removes the resting magnesium block of the NMDA receptor channel, allowing calcium influx and triggering intracellular signalling cascades that increase synaptic strength and neuronal excitability; NMDA receptor antagonists (e.g. ketamine) can attenuate or prevent central sensitisation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Central sensitisation, by definition, involves <strong>changes within the central nervous system</strong> (spinal cord dorsal horn and supraspinal structures) &mdash; increased neuronal excitability, expanded receptive fields, and reduced inhibitory tone &mdash; rather than being confined to peripheral changes (which instead describe peripheral sensitisation, a distinct process).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — A hallmark clinical feature of central sensitisation is that it can produce pain in response to normally non-painful stimuli (<strong>allodynia</strong>) and exaggerated pain to normally painful stimuli (<strong>hyperalgesia</strong>), extending beyond the site of the original injury (secondary hyperalgesia).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Central sensitisation typically develops in response to intense, repeated, or sustained nociceptive afferent input, with the temporal summation of C-fibre input (as seen in wind-up) considered an important triggering/early contributory mechanism leading to the more persistent state of central sensitisation.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Central sensitisation</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Definition</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Central sensitisation is an increase in the excitability and synaptic efficacy of neurons within central nociceptive pathways (spinal dorsal horn and supraspinal structures).</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">It happens following intense, repetitive, or sustained peripheral nociceptive input. It results in pain responses that are amplified, prolonged, and spatially extended beyond what the original stimulus/injury would predict.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Triggering Process</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>#</th><th>Stage</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Intense / sustained nociceptor activation</strong></td><td>From tissue injury or inflammation, causing repeated, high-frequency C-fibre afferent discharge into the dorsal horn.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Glutamate and neuropeptide release</strong></td><td>Presynaptic terminals of primary afferents release glutamate (acting on AMPA and NMDA receptors) along with co-transmitters substance P (acting on NK1 receptors) and calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Wind-up</strong></td><td>Repeated C-fibre stimulation at constant intensity produces a progressive, frequency-dependent increase in dorsal horn neuron output at the <em>same</em> synapse; this is a short-lived electrophysiological precursor/contributor rather than central sensitisation itself.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Key Molecular Mechanism &mdash; NMDA Receptor Unblocking</th></tr><tr><td>At rest, the NMDA receptor channel is blocked by <strong>magnesium (Mg<sup>2+</sup>)</strong> in a voltage-dependent manner.</td></tr><tr><td>Sustained depolarisation (from repetitive AMPA/NK1 receptor activation via glutamate/substance P) relieves this Mg<sup>2+</sup> block.</td></tr><tr><td>This allows <strong>calcium influx</strong> through the now-unblocked NMDA receptor.</td></tr><tr><td>Intracellular calcium activates second-messenger cascades (e.g. <strong>protein kinase C, CaMKII</strong>), leading to: phosphorylation of AMPA and NMDA receptors &rarr; increased receptor sensitivity/conductance; increased trafficking/insertion of AMPA receptors into the postsynaptic membrane &rarr; increased synaptic strength; activation of immediate early genes (e.g. c-fos) &rarr; longer-term transcriptional changes supporting sustained sensitisation.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Additional Contributing Mechanisms</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Loss of inhibitory tone (disinhibition)</strong></td><td>Reduction in GABAergic and glycinergic inhibitory interneuron activity in the dorsal horn, removing normal \"braking\" control on nociceptive transmission.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Microglial and astrocyte activation</strong></td><td>Release of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, IL-6) and other mediators that further enhance neuronal excitability &mdash; an important contributor to the transition from acute to chronic/neuropathic pain states.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Expansion of receptive fields</strong></td><td>Dorsal horn neurons begin responding to input from a wider area than their original receptive field, contributing to spread of pain beyond the original injury site.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduced activation threshold</strong></td><td>Neurons respond to previously subthreshold (non-noxious) input.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Manifestations</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Allodynia</strong></td><td>Pain from normally non-painful stimuli (e.g. light touch).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hyperalgesia</strong></td><td>Exaggerated pain response to normally painful stimuli.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Secondary hyperalgesia</strong></td><td>Pain sensitivity extending to undamaged tissue surrounding the original injury site.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Temporal summation</strong></td><td>Increasing pain perception with repeated stimuli of constant intensity (clinical correlate of wind-up).</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Clinical / Pharmacological Relevance</th></tr><tr><td><strong>NMDA receptor antagonists</strong> (ketamine, magnesium, methadone) can attenuate or help prevent the development of central sensitisation &mdash; the rationale for their use in chronic pain and opioid-tolerant states.</td></tr><tr><td>Central sensitisation is a key mechanism underlying <strong>chronic pain states</strong> where pain persists or amplifies beyond expected tissue healing time, and is central to understanding conditions such as fibromyalgia, chronic low back pain, and neuropathic pain syndromes.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguished from <strong>peripheral sensitisation</strong>, which is increased sensitivity of the peripheral nervous system to stimuli &mdash; lowered threshold/increased responsiveness of the primary nociceptor terminal itself (via the \"inflammatory soup\" &mdash; prostaglandins, bradykinin, ATP, potassium, etc.). The two processes are complementary but occur at different anatomical sites.</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_conventional_tens_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":76,"stem":"Regarding conventional TENS (Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation):","choices":["Typical frequency is 50–100 Hz","Typical pulse width is 50–200 milliseconds","Contraindicated in patients with epilepsy","Works via modulation of spinal GABA release","Contraindicated in patients with pacemakers"],"correct":[true,false,false,false,true],"explanation":"<p>Conventional TENS uses high-frequency, low-intensity stimulation to preferentially activate large-diameter A&beta; fibres, producing analgesia mainly through the spinal gate-control mechanism and opioid-mediated pathways, and understanding its parameters and safety profile is frequently tested.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Conventional TENS typically uses a <strong>high frequency of 50&ndash;100 Hz</strong>, delivered at low intensity, producing a comfortable tingling/buzzing sensation without visible muscle contraction.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — The typical pulse <strong>width</strong> (duration) for conventional TENS is in the range of <strong>50&ndash;200 microseconds</strong>, not milliseconds &mdash; this is a common unit-based exam trap; a pulse width of milliseconds would be several orders of magnitude too long and outside standard TENS parameters.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — TENS manufacturers list cardiac pacemakers, pregnancy, and epilepsy as contraindications largely because it may be difficult to exclude TENS as a cause of a problem from a legal perspective. In practice, TENS can be used safely in patients with epilepsy; there is no established mechanism by which peripheral transcutaneous electrical stimulation would provoke seizure activity. Recognised contraindications instead include use over the carotid sinus, in patients with cardiac pacemakers/implantable defibrillators, and over the pregnant abdomen.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — Conventional TENS does <strong>not</strong> work primarily through GABA modulation; its main proposed mechanisms are the <strong>gate control theory</strong> (activation of large A&beta; fibres inhibiting nociceptive transmission at the dorsal horn) and spinal <strong>delta-opioid receptor</strong> activation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — TENS is contraindicated (or requires specialist caution) in patients with cardiac pacemakers or implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (ICDs), due to the risk of electrical interference with device function; TENS electrodes should also not be placed near the device itself even where cautious use is considered acceptable.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">TENS</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation (TENS) is a non-invasive, low-cost, patient-controlled method of pain relief. A small battery-powered pulse generator delivers electrical pulses to the skin through adhesive surface electrodes.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Parameters that can be modified on a TENS machine include pulse duration (in microseconds), pulse frequency (in Hz or pulses/sec) and pulse amplitude (in milliamperes).</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Types of TENS</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Frequency</th><th>Intensity</th><th>Fibres activated</th><th>Proposed mechanism</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Conventional TENS</strong></td><td>High (~50&ndash;100 Hz)</td><td>Low &mdash; comfortable tingling, no visible contraction</td><td>Large-diameter A&beta; afferents</td><td>Segmental gate-control inhibition of dorsal horn transmission; spinal delta-opioid receptor involvement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Acupuncture-like TENS (AL-TENS)</strong></td><td>Low (~2&ndash;4 Hz)</td><td>High &mdash; visible muscle twitching</td><td>Smaller A&delta; afferents / motor efferents</td><td>Extrasegmental, opioid-mediated (mu-receptor) analgesia via activation of descending inhibitory pathways</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Intense TENS</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Very high &mdash; at or near the limit of tolerability</td><td>Small-diameter A&delta; (and some C) fibres</td><td>Counter-irritation / hyperstimulation analgesia; typically used briefly for short, painful procedures (e.g. wound dressing changes, injections) rather than continuously, as tolerance to the high intensity limits prolonged application</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">TENS Contraindications</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Contraindication / precaution</th><th>Reason</th></tr><tr><td>Cardiac implantable devices</td><td>Pacemakers and implantable cardioverter-defibrillators &mdash; electrical interference can disrupt device sensing/pacing.</td></tr><tr><td>Over the carotid sinus</td><td>Risk of provoking a vasovagal/hypotensive response.</td></tr><tr><td>Over the anterior neck / larynx</td><td>Risk of laryngospasm or interference with airway reflexes.</td></tr><tr><td>Pregnancy</td><td>Avoid placement over the abdomen or lower back, particularly in early pregnancy, due to theoretical uterine stimulation risk (direct evidence of harm is limited).</td></tr><tr><td>Active malignancy at the treatment site</td><td>Theoretical concern regarding electrical stimulation of tumour tissue, though evidence is limited.</td></tr><tr><td>Broken/irritated skin, adhesive allergy</td><td>Local skin precautions.</td></tr><tr><td>Driving, operating machinery, or sleep</td><td>Devices should never be applied then, given the reliance on patient feedback for safe intensity titration.</td></tr><tr><td>Dysaesthesia / diminished sensation</td><td>TENS should not be applied to skin with diminished sensation &mdash; nerve damage is likely to reduce effectiveness and the patient may be unaware that high-intensity currents are causing skin irritation. Caution with mechanical allodynia, as TENS may exacerbate the pain.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Effectiveness</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Clinical experience suggests that TENS may relieve any type of chronic pain, yet systematic reviews are inconclusive for chronic pain, low back pain, cancer-related pain, rheumatoid arthritis of the hand, post-stroke shoulder pain, whiplash and mechanical neck disorders, and chronic recurrent headache. Meta-analyses for osteoarthritic knee pain and for chronic musculoskeletal pain have been more positive, suggesting that TENS is superior to sham TENS for pain and stiffness. Non-randomised controlled clinical trials have found benefit for many types of chronic pain.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">NICE guidance does <strong>not</strong> recommend routinely offering TENS for chronic low back pain.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_amitriptyline_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":77,"stem":"Regarding amitriptyline:","choices":["Blocks sodium channels within the CNS","Works via a potassium channel-mediated mechanism","Has low anticholinergic properties","Is beneficial for depression at low doses","Inhibits reuptake of both serotonin and noradrenaline"],"correct":[true,false,false,false,true],"explanation":"<p>Amitriptyline is a tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) with a broad, multimodal mechanism of action relevant to both its analgesic and antidepressant effects, but also carries a significant side-effect burden that limits its use, particularly in older or frailer patients.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Amitriptyline blocks <strong>voltage-gated sodium channels</strong> both peripherally and centrally, contributing a local anaesthetic-like, membrane-stabilising effect that is thought to underlie part of its analgesic action in neuropathic pain, independent of its monoamine reuptake inhibition.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Potassium channel blockade is not a recognised principal mechanism of amitriptyline''s action; its main mechanisms are monoamine reuptake inhibition and sodium channel blockade, along with antagonism at several other receptors.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Amitriptyline has <strong>high (not low) anticholinergic properties</strong>, causing recognised antimuscarinic side effects such as dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, and confusion &mdash; particularly problematic in elderly patients, and a key reason for caution/avoidance in this population.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — Amitriptyline''s analgesic effect for neuropathic pain occurs at <strong>low doses</strong> (typically 10&ndash;75 mg), well below the doses required for an <strong>antidepressant</strong> effect (typically 150 mg or more).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Amitriptyline acts as a <strong>non-selective monoamine reuptake inhibitor</strong>, inhibiting reuptake of both <strong>serotonin and noradrenaline</strong> at the presynaptic terminal, enhancing descending inhibitory pain pathways &mdash; this is central to both its analgesic and antidepressant mechanisms.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Amitriptyline</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Class:</strong> Tricyclic antidepressant (TCA)</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-selective monoamine reuptake inhibitor</strong></td><td>Blocks presynaptic reuptake of both serotonin (5-HT) and noradrenaline, enhancing descending inhibitory pain pathways and mood regulation.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sodium channel blockade (central &amp; peripheral)</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like, membrane-stabilising effect contributing to analgesia, independent of monoamine effects.</td></tr><tr><td>Muscarinic (antimuscarinic) antagonism</td><td>High affinity &mdash; drives much of the side-effect profile.</td></tr><tr><td>Histamine H1 antagonism</td><td>Sedative effect.</td></tr><tr><td>Alpha-1 adrenergic antagonism</td><td>Postural hypotension.</td></tr><tr><td>NMDA receptor antagonism</td><td>Some activity, contributing to the analgesic effect.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Dose-Dependent Effect</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Indication</th><th>Typical dose</th><th>Onset</th></tr><tr><td>Neuropathic pain</td><td>Low dose (10&ndash;50 mg, often at night)</td><td>Days to ~2 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Depression</td><td>Higher dose (150 mg+)</td><td>2&ndash;4 weeks</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Analgesic benefit occurs independently of, and at doses well below, those needed for antidepressant effect.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Pharmacokinetics</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>Absorption</td><td>Well absorbed orally; significant first-pass hepatic metabolism, giving relatively low and variable oral bioavailability.</td></tr><tr><td>Metabolism</td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP2D6 (and CYP1A2, CYP3A4) &rarr; demethylation to nortriptyline, itself a pharmacologically active tricyclic antidepressant/analgesic (more noradrenaline-selective than the parent drug).</td></tr><tr><td>Protein binding</td><td>Highly protein-bound (~95%).</td></tr><tr><td>Volume of distribution</td><td>Large (highly lipophilic, extensive tissue distribution) &mdash; relevant to the difficulty of dialysing it out in overdose.</td></tr><tr><td>Half-life</td><td>Long and variable, roughly 10&ndash;28 hours (contributes to once-daily dosing, typically at night due to sedation).</td></tr><tr><td>Excretion</td><td>Renal excretion of (conjugated) metabolites, with some faecal elimination.</td></tr><tr><td>Genetic variability</td><td>CYP2D6 polymorphism causes significant interpatient variability &mdash; poor metabolisers may accumulate higher levels at standard doses (increasing side-effect/toxicity risk); ultra-rapid metabolisers may have reduced efficacy at standard doses.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Clinical Uses</th></tr><tr><td>Neuropathic pain (first/second-line agent per NICE guidance)</td></tr><tr><td>Chronic pain syndromes (e.g. fibromyalgia &mdash; off-label)</td></tr><tr><td>Migraine prophylaxis</td></tr><tr><td>Depression (higher dose)</td></tr><tr><td>Insomnia (off-label, low dose, due to sedative H1 antagonism)</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Side Effects (largely receptor-driven)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>System</th><th>Effects</th></tr><tr><td>Anticholinergic</td><td>Dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, confusion (especially in the elderly).</td></tr><tr><td>Antihistaminergic</td><td>Sedation, weight gain.</td></tr><tr><td>Alpha-1 blockade</td><td>Postural hypotension.</td></tr><tr><td>Cardiac</td><td>QTc prolongation, arrhythmia risk (sodium/potassium channel effects) &mdash; caution in cardiac disease.</td></tr><tr><td>CNS</td><td>Lowers the seizure threshold.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Contraindications / Cautions</th></tr><tr><td>Recent myocardial infarction</td></tr><tr><td>Arrhythmias, heart block</td></tr><tr><td>Severe hepatic impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Concurrent MAOI use (risk of serotonin syndrome / hypertensive crisis)</td></tr><tr><td>Caution in elderly patients (anticholinergic burden, falls risk, sedation)</td></tr><tr><td>Caution in narrow-angle glaucoma and urinary retention (e.g. prostatic hypertrophy)</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Overdose</th></tr><tr><td>Classic and dangerous in TCA overdose: cardiotoxicity (sodium channel blockade &rarr; wide QRS, arrhythmias), seizures, severe anticholinergic toxicity, coma.</td></tr><tr><td>Sodium bicarbonate is used in the management of TCA-induced cardiotoxicity (widened QRS).</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_botulinum_toxin_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":78,"stem":"Regarding botulinum toxin:","choices":["Should be stored at 18°C","Onset of clinical effect typically takes 1–2 weeks","Licensed for use in episodic migraine","Can cause flu-like symptoms","Acts as an agonist at the acetylcholine receptor"],"correct":[false,true,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>Botulinum toxin is a potent neurotoxin used therapeutically in several pain and movement disorder conditions, with specific storage, onset, licensing, and mechanistic properties that are frequently tested.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Botulinum toxin requires <strong>refrigerated storage</strong>, typically between <strong>2&ndash;8&deg;C</strong> (in a fridge), not at 18&deg;C; it must be protected from heat and light to preserve potency, and once reconstituted has a limited shelf life requiring prompt use.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — The clinical effect of botulinum toxin typically has a <strong>delayed onset</strong>, becoming apparent over <strong>1&ndash;2 weeks</strong> following injection, as it takes time for the toxin to be internalised and to block neurotransmitter release at the neuromuscular junction / nerve terminal.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Botulinum toxin is licensed for <strong>chronic migraine</strong> (headache on &ge;15 days/month), <strong>not episodic migraine</strong>, where the evidence does not support its use.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Flu-like symptoms (malaise, myalgia, low-grade fever) are a recognised, generally mild and self-limiting side effect following botulinum toxin injection.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Botulinum toxin does <strong>not</strong> act as an agonist at the acetylcholine receptor &mdash; it works by cleaving <strong>SNARE proteins</strong> (e.g. SNAP-25) required for vesicular fusion, thereby preventing acetylcholine release from the presynaptic nerve terminal at the neuromuscular junction, producing a chemodenervation effect (flaccid paralysis) rather than receptor agonism.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Botulinum Toxin</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Class:</strong> Neurotoxin produced by <em>Clostridium botulinum</em>; multiple serotypes exist (A&ndash;G), with type A (onabotulinumtoxinA / BoNT-A) most widely used clinically.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Mechanism of Action</th></tr><tr><td>Toxin is taken up at the presynaptic nerve terminal (peripheral cholinergic synapses, including the neuromuscular junction).</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaves <strong>SNARE proteins</strong> (SNAP-25 for type A; VAMP/synaptobrevin for type B) required for vesicle docking and fusion.</td></tr><tr><td>This <strong>prevents acetylcholine release</strong> from the presynaptic terminal, producing <strong>chemodenervation</strong> &mdash; a temporary, reversible flaccid paralysis of the affected muscle, or reduced glandular secretion where used for sweat glands.</td></tr><tr><td>Also has emerging evidence of a direct <strong>antinociceptive effect</strong> independent of muscle paralysis, via inhibition of release of pain-related neuropeptides (substance P, CGRP, glutamate) from sensory nerve terminals &mdash; relevant to its analgesic use (e.g. chronic migraine).</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Pharmacokinetics / Handling</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>Refrigerated (2&ndash;8&deg;C), protected from light; must not be frozen.</td></tr><tr><td>Reconstituted shelf life</td><td>Limited &mdash; should be used promptly (typically within a few hours, per manufacturer guidance).</td></tr><tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Acts locally at the injection site with minimal systemic absorption at standard therapeutic doses.</td></tr><tr><td>Onset of effect</td><td>Delayed, typically 1&ndash;2 weeks.</td></tr><tr><td>Duration of effect</td><td>Typically 3&ndash;4 months, as new nerve terminal sprouting and SNARE protein turnover restore neurotransmission, necessitating repeat injections for sustained benefit.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Indications</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Area</th><th>Uses</th></tr><tr><td>Pain medicine</td><td>Chronic migraine (&ge;15 headache days/month, &ge;8 of which are migrainous) &mdash; NOT licensed for episodic migraine; myofascial pain syndrome / trigger point injections (off-label); emerging/off-label interest in neuropathic pain, complex regional pain syndrome, and post-herpetic neuralgia.</td></tr><tr><td>Neurology / movement disorders</td><td>Spasticity (post-stroke, cerebral palsy, multiple sclerosis); cervical dystonia; blepharospasm; hemifacial spasm.</td></tr><tr><td>Other established uses</td><td>Hyperhidrosis (axillary, palmar); detrusor overactivity / overactive bladder; sialorrhoea; achalasia (oesophageal sphincter injection); cosmetic use (glabellar lines, crow''s feet).</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Side Effects</th></tr><tr><td>Flu-like symptoms (malaise, myalgia, mild fever) &mdash; generally mild, self-limiting.</td></tr><tr><td>Localised pain / bruising at the injection site.</td></tr><tr><td>Unintended weakness of adjacent muscles (spread of effect) &mdash; e.g. ptosis with facial injections, dysphagia with cervical dystonia treatment.</td></tr><tr><td>Rarely, systemic spread causing generalised weakness (particularly at high doses or in patients with neuromuscular junction disorders).</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Contraindications</th></tr><tr><td>Known hypersensitivity to botulinum toxin or formulation components.</td></tr><tr><td>Infection at the proposed injection site.</td></tr><tr><td>Neuromuscular junction disorders (e.g. myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome) &mdash; increased risk of profound/generalised weakness.</td></tr><tr><td>Pregnancy and breastfeeding (generally avoided, limited safety data).</td></tr><tr><td>Caution with concurrent aminoglycoside use (can potentiate neuromuscular blockade).</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_mri_physics_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":79,"stem":"MRI:","choices":["Works through isotopes of water","T1-weighted imaging is better than T2 for grey-white matter differentiation","T1-weighted imaging is better than T2 for detecting oedema","T2-weighted imaging is better than T1 for assessing CSF","T1 and T2 relaxation times are related to the radiofrequency pulse frequency used"],"correct":[false,true,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>MRI generates images based on the behaviour of hydrogen protons (not isotopes) within a strong magnetic field, and the relative weighting of the sequence (T1 vs T2) determines how different tissue types and pathologies are best visualised.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — MRI works through the magnetic properties of <strong>hydrogen protons</strong> (particularly abundant in water and fat molecules) aligning within a strong external magnetic field and then relaxing back to equilibrium after a radiofrequency pulse.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — <strong>T1-weighted</strong> images provide superior anatomical detail and are generally considered better for <strong>grey-white matter differentiation</strong>, with grey matter appearing darker and white matter appearing brighter due to differing fat/myelin content affecting T1 relaxation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — <strong>T2-weighted</strong> imaging is more sensitive for detecting <strong>oedema</strong>, as tissue/fluid with high water content appears bright on T2 sequences, making it the preferred sequence for identifying oedematous or inflammatory pathology.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — <strong>T2-weighted</strong> imaging is better for assessing <strong>CSF</strong> and fluid-containing structures, as CSF appears bright (hyperintense) on T2 sequences, in contrast to its dark appearance on T1-weighted images.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — T1 and T2 are <strong>relaxation times</strong> (measured in milliseconds) describing how quickly protons return to equilibrium after an RF pulse &mdash; T1 (longitudinal / spin-lattice relaxation) and T2 (transverse / spin-spin relaxation). They are intrinsic tissue properties related to the magnetic environment and interactions between protons and surrounding molecules, not directly defined by or equivalent to the radiofrequency pulse frequency itself.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">MRI</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"position:relative;width:100%;padding-top:56.25%;border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;background:#000\">\n          <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/jLnuPKhKXVM?start=302\" title=\"How MRI Works\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;border:0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen></iframe>\n        </div><p style=\"margin:14px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">MRI exploits the magnetic behaviour of <strong>hydrogen protons</strong> (abundant in water and fat throughout the body) when placed in a strong external magnetic field. Protons align with the field, and a radiofrequency (RF) pulse is applied to temporarily knock them out of alignment. As protons \"relax\" back to equilibrium after the RF pulse is switched off, they emit signals that are detected and reconstructed into an image.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Key Relaxation Parameters</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Definition</th></tr><tr><td><strong>T1</strong></td><td>Time for longitudinal magnetisation to recover (protons realign with the main magnetic field).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>T2</strong></td><td>Time for transverse magnetisation to decay (protons lose phase coherence with each other).</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Different tissues have characteristic T1 and T2 relaxation times based on their molecular environment (fat content, water content, protein binding), which is the basis of tissue contrast in MRI.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">T1 vs T2 Weighted Imaging</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>T1-weighted</th><th>T2-weighted</th></tr><tr><td>Fat</td><td>Bright</td><td>Intermediate/bright (less so than T1)</td></tr><tr><td>Water / CSF / oedema</td><td>Dark</td><td>Bright</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Anatomical detail, grey-white matter differentiation</td><td>Pathology detection &mdash; oedema, inflammation, tumours, CSF assessment</td></tr><tr><td>Mnemonic</td><td>\"T1 = ana<strong>T</strong>omy\"</td><td>\"T2 = pathology / wa<strong>T</strong>er\"</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"text-align:center\"><img src=\"images/mri-t1-t2-brain-comparison.png\" alt=\"Axial brain MRI comparison — T1-weighted (anatomy/structure: white matter bright, CSF dark) versus T2-weighted (pathology/disease: CSF bright, white matter dark grey), with a feature table for CSF, white matter, fat and oedema/tumour\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:0;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"text-align:center\"><img src=\"images/mri-t1-t2-lumbar-spine.png\" alt=\"Sagittal lumbar spine MRI — T1-weighted and T2-weighted unlabelled images with an annotated reference (bone marrow, spinal cord, CSF, epidural fat, subcutaneous fat, nucleus pulposus, bone cortex) and a table of T1 vs T2 signal for fat, CSF and bone\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:0;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Common Sequences (Beyond Basic T1/T2)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Sequence</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>STIR</strong> (Short Tau Inversion Recovery)</td><td>Fat-suppression sequence; highly sensitive for oedema / bone marrow pathology (commonly used in spinal imaging for pain medicine).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FLAIR</strong> (Fluid-Attenuated Inversion Recovery)</td><td>Suppresses CSF signal while retaining sensitivity to oedema/lesions &mdash; useful in brain imaging (e.g. MS plaques).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI)</strong></td><td>Detects restricted water molecule diffusion &mdash; highly sensitive for acute ischaemic stroke.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contrast-enhanced MRI</strong> (gadolinium-based)</td><td>Highlights areas of blood-brain barrier breakdown, inflammation, tumour, or infection (e.g. helps distinguish recurrent disc herniation from post-surgical scar tissue in the spine).</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_imaging_modalities_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-05","rotationIndex":80,"stem":"Regarding imaging modalities:","choices":["CT works through the spin of molecules","Functional MRI (fMRI) helps detect changes in blood flow","MRI encephalogram can detect changes over a very short period","PET involves injection of a radioactive tracer that is taken up by metabolically active cells","Ultrasound imaging works via the piezoelectric effect"],"correct":[false,true,true,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Different imaging modalities rely on fundamentally different physical principles &mdash; X-ray attenuation, proton behaviour in a magnetic field, magnetic fields generated by neuronal activity, radiotracer uptake, and the piezoelectric effect &mdash; and distinguishing them is frequently tested.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — CT (Computed Tomography) works through <strong>differential attenuation of X-rays</strong> as they pass through tissues of varying density, reconstructed mathematically into cross-sectional images &mdash; it does <strong>not</strong> rely on the spin of molecules/protons, which is instead the underlying principle of MRI.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Functional MRI (fMRI) detects changes in <strong>blood flow and oxygenation</strong> (the BOLD signal &mdash; Blood Oxygen Level Dependent contrast), reflecting regional neuronal activity, since active brain regions receive increased oxygenated blood flow relative to oxygen extraction, altering the local magnetic signal.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — Magnetoencephalography (MEG) directly measures the tiny magnetic fields produced by neuronal electrical activity, allowing detection of functional brain changes over very short (millisecond) time periods, offering excellent temporal resolution (superior to fMRI, which has better spatial but poorer temporal resolution).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — PET (Positron Emission Tomography) involves injecting a <strong>radioactive tracer</strong> (commonly a radiolabelled glucose analogue, FDG) which is preferentially taken up by <strong>metabolically active cells</strong> (e.g. tumour cells, active neurons); the emitted positrons are detected to construct functional/metabolic images, often combined with CT (PET-CT) for anatomical correlation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — Ultrasound generates images using the <strong>piezoelectric effect</strong> &mdash; a transducer crystal converts electrical energy into high-frequency sound waves, which are reflected differentially by tissue interfaces and reconverted into electrical signals to form an image; unlike CT/MRI, no ionising radiation or strong magnetic field is involved.</p>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;

